\par\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 Broadway Actors Reach a Deal, but Musicians May Still Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Paulson \par \uc2 491 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Broadway League and Actors' Equity Association reached a tentative agreement early Saturday. Union members must decide whether to ratify it.\par \par \uc2 The union representing Broadway's actors and stage managers reached a tentative agreement with the industry's commercial producers early Saturday morning, but a potential strike still looms because musicians have yet to make a deal.\par \par \uc2 Details of the agreement between \uc2 Actors' Equity Association\uc2  and the Broadway League, which represents producers and theater owners, were not immediately available. The union had been seeking higher wages and greater health care benefits.\par \par \uc2 The previous Equity contract expired on Sept. 28, and the union had authorized its leaders to call a strike if necessary, putting the industry on edge.\par \par \uc2 ''After a marathon mediation session lasting until 6 a.m., Equity and the Broadway League have reached a tentative agreement on the production contract,'' the union's executive director, Al Vincent Jr., said in a statement, referring to the contract that governs work by actors and stage managers working on for-profit Broadway shows.\par \par \uc2 ''This three-year agreement,'' Vincent added, ''saves the Equity-League Health Fund while also making strides in our other priorities including scheduling and physical therapy access.''\par \par \uc2 The League also welcomed the progress.\par \par \uc2 ''We're pleased to have a reached a tentative agreement on terms both parties are comfortable with,'' Jason Laks, the League's president, said in a statement. ''Our focus has been negotiating in good faith at the bargaining table to reach a fair contract, and we thank our mediators for their diligent work bringing us together.''\par \par \uc2 The union will next share details of the agreement with its national council, which will vote on whether to recommend ratification. If it does, the agreement will be voted on by a subset of the union's membership. Although Equity has more than 51,000 members, only those who have worked on Broadway since 2019 are eligible to vote on the contract. The union estimates that number at just under 4,000 members.\par \par \uc2 American Federation of Musicians Local 802, which represents musicians who work on Broadway, continues to warn that its members could walk out if they do not reach their own deal with the League. That contract expired on Aug. 31.\par \par \uc2 The musicians are also hoping for higher wages and improved health care benefits, and they want to fend off an effort by producers to reduce compensation for instrumentalists who are paid additional money to take on some administrative tasks.\par \par \uc2 The musicians' union is seeking to meet again with the League over the coming week.\par \par \uc2 ''We are thankful that our brothers and sisters in labor at Actors' Equity have reached an agreement,'' Robert Suttmann, the president of the musicians' union, said in a statement. ''Local 802 is still in negotiation for a fair contract, and everything remains on the table, including a strike.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251020elak0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
