\par\par \uc2 Money and Business/Financial Desk; SECTBU\par \b \uc2 7.5%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lora Kelley \par \uc2 424 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate for Black Americans.\par \par \uc2 The joblessness rate for Black workers has ticked up in the past four months, to 7.5 percent from 6 percent. At the same time, the unemployment rate for white workers has dipped slightly to 3.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 The decline in Black employment, after a historically strong labor market, is a worrisome sign: During economic downturns, marginalized groups often see a larger hit to employment and take longer to recover, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 's Lydia DePillis reported.\par \par \uc2 A few factors have recently come together to fuel higher joblessness among Black Americans. Federal layoffs have hit Black workers disproportionately hard -- and the cuts to federal jobs remove a pathway to employment for younger workers. The Trump administration's assault on diversity, equity and inclusion initiatives -- in his first days in office, President Trump ordered employers to halt racial equity policies -- is adding to the challenges.\par \par \uc2 The slowdown in employment follows a strong few years for Black workers. During the first Trump administration, Black unemployment rates were low, though still twice the rate for white people. Federal subsidies helped soften the blow when the pandemic diminished in-person jobs, and soon wages grew quickly and Black household wealth rose to historic highs.\par \par \uc2 But last year, as pandemic-era subsidies ran out, inflation persisted and hiring slowed, Black households were the only racial group for which the poverty rate rose and median income fell, according to Census Bureau data. Now, the once-strong labor market for Black workers has been weakened.\par \par \uc2 ''There's been such a rapid shift in policy, rather than something cyclical or structural about the economy,'' \uc2 Valerie Wilson\uc2 , who directs the program on race, ethnicity and the economy at the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , a left-leaning think tank, told Ms. DePillis. She called the recent changes ''out of the ordinary.''\par \par \uc2 ''This offers more choice to creators and unlocks a completely new distribution opportunity.''\par \par \uc2 Lauren Smith, a vice president at Netflix, on the company's move into video podcasting.\par \par \uc2 ''I feel like I'm more trying to build a frat house, and you don't commute to a frat house.''\par \par \uc2 Roy Lee, whose artificial intelligence start-up, Cluely, has leased apartments for employees a minute walk from its office in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 ''It doesn't matter to us at all if a building has a pool table or fancy food.''\par \par \uc2 Ken Podziba, chief executive of Bike New York, of his search for office space for his team.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251019elaj00067\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
