\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Lawyers Seek To Investigate Insurers' Ties To President\b0\par\par\uc2 By Benjamin Weiser \par \uc2 736 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lawyers for Luigi Mangione, charged with killing \uc2 UnitedHealthcare\uc2 's chief executive, are asking for details about a reported meeting between the White House and an executive with the insurer.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the man accused of assassinating the chief executive of \uc2 UnitedHealthcare\uc2 , asked a federal judge on Friday to let them investigate whether the Justice Department and company officials have cooperated to ''malign and prejudice'' their client.\par \par \uc2 The request is the latest turn in a festering dispute that began after Trump administration officials said repeatedly that the man, Luigi Mangione is guilty. The judge in the case has said the comments may violate her order prohibiting the parties from making statements that could affect Mr. Mangione's ability to receive a fair trial.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mangione faces a murder charge in the killing of the chief executive, \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 , who was gunned down last December shortly before an investors' meeting at a Midtown Manhattan hotel. Mr. Mangione has pleaded not guilty to the charge, which carries a potential death penalty.\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Mangione was arrested a few days after the killing, the authorities said he was carrying writings denouncing the ''parasitic'' U.S. insurance industry and the nation's system of for-profit health care.\par \par \uc2 His lawyers asked in their filing on Friday that the judge, Margaret Garnett of Federal District Court in Manhattan, allow them to seek evidence related to reported discussions between \uc2 UnitedHealthcare\uc2 's parent, \uc2 UnitedHealth Group\uc2 , and administration officials.\par \par \uc2 The lawyers cited a recent Wall Street Journal article that said that \uc2 UnitedHealth Group\uc2 's chief executive, \uc2 Stephen J. Hemsley\uc2 , had met with President Trump's chief of staff, Susie Wiles, ''to discuss Medicare and other issues.'' (The Journal has also reported that \uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2  was facing a criminal investigation into a key Medicare business.)\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 UnitedHealth\uc2 's continued financial viability depends upon maintaining the narrative that Mr. Mangione -- rather than the company or its business practices -- is the villain,'' Mr. Mangione's lawyers wrote. They said they wanted to determine whether the ''other issues'' mentioned in the Journal article ''precipitated coordinated efforts by the White House and the Department of Justice to malign and prejudice Mr. Mangione.''\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 UnitedHealthcare\uc2  spokesman did not respond to a request for comment, nor did a spokesman for the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mangione's lawyers, led by Karen Friedman Agnifilo, also argued in the filing that the government had not answered a question Judge Garnett had posed earlier: How did two Justice Department officials come to make social media posts about Mr. Mangione's guilt given her directive that the parties not speak publicly about the case.\par \par \uc2 The defense lawyers asked the judge to order the government to turn over internal government documents, emails and other communications ''that will answer the question.''\par \par \uc2 The latest chapter in the dispute began last month when Mr. Trump told Fox News that Mr. Mangione ''shot someone in the back as clear as you're looking at me.'' A social media account affiliated with the White House posted the statement, which a Justice Department spokesman, Chad Gilmartin, reposted with the added comment that the president ''is absolutely right."\par \par \uc2 Two days later, another Justice Department official reposted the statement on his account.\par \par \uc2 Judge Garnett issued the directive that the parties not discuss the case in late April, after a flurry of statements by Attorney General Pam Bondi about the government's decision to seek capital punishment.\par \par \uc2 The judge told the U.S. attorney's office in Manhattan to convey her order about refraining from public commentary to Ms. Bondi and her subordinates at the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 Last month, after the two Justice Department officials posted Mr. Trump's statement on social media, Judge Garnett requested the explanation for how that had happened. She said future violations could result in sanctions or other penalties.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. attorney's office responded on Oct. 8, arguing that the two officials who made the posts were not members of the prosecution team; did not report to the prosecutors; and had no ''operational role'' in the case.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the office said, the statements had not violated court rules because they were made by persons ''not associated with this matter.'' The office also said that upon becoming aware of the posts, the department promptly directed that they be removed.\par \par \uc2 Hurubie Meko contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Hurubie Meko contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251018elai0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
