\par\par \uc2 YOUR MONEY ADVISER\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 There's a Battle Over Your 401(k) Accounts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ann Carrns \par \uc2 1,843 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Financial tech companies are connecting outside financial advisers to your retirement accounts, and at least one investment firm, \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 , is resisting.\par \par \uc2 For years, employees with workplace retirement plans like 401(k)s were largely expected to choose investments from a menu provided by the investment companies hired by their employer and then manage the investments on their own.\par \par \uc2 But now, upstart financial technology firms are connecting outside financial advisers to employer-sponsored plans, allowing the advisers to take steps like rebalancing accounts on behalf of their clients.\par \par \uc2 At least one investment company is resisting the financial tech firms' moves. And both employees and the outside advisers are finding themselves caught in a tug of war over who can manage these retirement accounts and how they do it.\par \par \uc2 Fidelity Investments\uc2 , the country's biggest 401(k) administrator, has been warning investors in these plans that they may lose online access to their accounts, and that it could even void certain customer protections, if they share their online credentials with firms offering such tools.\par \par \uc2 One investor who was temporarily locked out of his account at \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  was Kelly Havins, 63, a grocery brand consultant in Phoenix. Mr. Havins said he had hired a financial adviser who offers 401(k) management through a service provided by a technology firm called \uc2 Pontera\uc2 . ''I don't have the time or the understanding'' to manage investments, he said.\par \par \uc2 In late August, Mr. Havins received a letter from \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 , saying that because he had shared his login with an outside provider, he had to contact the company to reset his credentials -- or risk being barred from online access to his 401(k).\par \par \uc2 ''I was surprised,'' Mr. Havins said. ''I thought it was a scam.''\par \par \uc2 On Sept. 4, with a deadline for losing access approaching, he called \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 . ''They said, 'We have to make sure it's you,''' he recalled, and asked him to email a copy of his driver's license. He declined. ''I said: 'I'm not comfortable with that. You know who I am,''' Mr. Havins said. ''And they said, 'Consider yourself locked out.'''\par \par \uc2 The next day, he said, he was unable to log into his account. He was eventually able to reset his credentials and regain access with the help of his financial adviser. But his account could be flagged again.\par \par \uc2 Many affected plan participants who contacted \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  to reset their credentials said they were unaware they had shared them, \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 What's different about the new 401(k) digital access tools?\par \par \uc2 Some earlier digital tools, like \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 's \uc2 eMoney\uc2  financial planning software, gave advisers ''read only'' access to workplace plans, allowing them to simply view data. \uc2 Pontera\uc2  and a handful of competitors go further, providing digital tools that allow advisers to take action within the 401(k), like rebalancing accounts to make sure investments reflect the client's goals and comfort with risk.\par \par \uc2 Investment advisers say the access is significant because it allows them to better coordinate holdings in workplace plans with a client's other investments when deciding how to allocate funds. That helps make sure the client does not have too much invested in stocks, for instance.\par \par \uc2 Pontera\uc2  promotes its service to advisers, who invite their clients to connect their accounts to \uc2 Pontera\uc2 's platform via a screen at the office or an email. The advisers can't see or gain access to the credentials, which are held by \uc2 Pontera\uc2 ; its system taps into \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  to carry out the adviser's instructions. \uc2 Pontera\uc2  charges the advisers a fee on the assets managed through its platform\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Pontera\uc2  has been a game changer,'' said Carl Szasz, founder of Verde Capital Management in Clarkston, Mich. Because he is a fiduciary -- meaning he must act in his clients' best interests -- he wants to include his clients' workplace accounts in their financial management plan, he said. ''I want to be able to act and do my job.''\par \par \uc2 The employer-based retirement market is vast. Americans held $13 trillion in all employer-based plans, including $9.3 trillion in 401(k) plans, according to midyear data from the \uc2 Investment Company Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What do workplace retirement plan administrators think of the tools?\par \par \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 , with more than 24 million plan participants in some 25,000 corporate plans, is wary of some of the new tools. The retirement giant had been warning for nearly a year that it would move to bar certain fintech firms from its employer-sponsored accounts because the firms tap into the accounts with user names and passwords shared with them by investors working with their clients.\par \par \uc2 ''Credential sharing presents security risks to our customers,'' \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  said in September 2024, ''particularly when it enables third parties to take high-risk actions, such as executing trades within the account.''\par \par \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  recently began making good on that warning, cutting off online access to savers if they shared their credentials with digital tools that gave their advisers access to their accounts.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Szasz said about 190 of his firm's clients with accounts at \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  were disconnected. Some have been able to reconnect, but others have not.\par \par \uc2 ''They're stopping people from accessing their own accounts,'' he said. ''We're stuck in the middle.''\par \par \uc2 He noted that \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  offered financial advisement through its own network, so it might have an incentive to limit access by outside advisers.\par \par \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  said in an email that its efforts to require participants to update their credentials weren't focusing on whether third-party advisers should have access to a participant's account. ''We are focused on how some advisers are gaining such access by using customer credentials,'' it said.\par \par \uc2 Other 401(k) administrators seem comfortable with \uc2 Pontera\uc2 . The firm, for instance, recently announced a ''collaboration'' with Manulife John Hancock Retirement.\par \par \uc2 ''We've done our due diligence,'' said Wayne Park, the retirement firm's chief executive, adding that ''their security is up to our standards.'' Having a secure digital connection, he said, is superior to old-school alternatives, like having clients deliver stacks of paper statements to their advisers.\par \par \uc2 Do the new tools comply with financial regulations?\par \par \uc2 Pontera\uc2  says its model, in which investors authorize it to act as their agent, fits into various regulatory frameworks, including \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  guidance on how financial advisers may gain access to workplace accounts. Advisers working with \uc2 Pontera\uc2  don't have access to a client's login credentials -- that is managed by \uc2 Pontera\uc2  -- and they can't withdraw or transfer funds or change beneficiaries, steps that would signal they had taken ''custody'' of the account.\par \par \uc2 ''Consumers have rights to ask that an agent perform an action for them,'' Zachary Pardes, a \uc2 Pontera\uc2  spokesman, said in an email. ''This is not a gray area.''\par \par \uc2 Yoav Zurel, \uc2 Pontera\uc2 's chief executive, said, ''This is what customers want, and it's their money.''\par \par \uc2 Asked for comment, the S.E.C. press office sent an email on Wednesday saying it was unable to respond because of the government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 Competing firms provide similar services but operate differently. Absolute Capital of Pittsburgh also helps financial advisers manage their clients' workplace retirement plans. But it has formal agreements with retirement plan custodians so when it taps into a customer's 401(k), the plan knows it is Absolute Capital. (With \uc2 Pontera\uc2 's model, the firm is logging in as the client.)\par \par \uc2 ''We come in through the front door,'' said Brenden Gebben, the company's chief executive. ''We're recognized as the authorized agent.''\par \par \uc2 Some state regulators, who generally oversee smaller advisory firms, have raised concerns, including those in Colorado. Some other states, like Rhode Island, have said using the tools is acceptable as long as advisers discuss them with clients and act in keeping with their fiduciary duties.\par \par \uc2 Pontera\uc2  acknowledged that it did not have a formal data-sharing agreement with \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 , but maintained that it wasn't required to have one, in part because plan participants grant the firm access to their information.\par \par \uc2 Pontera\uc2  says client credentials are stored in a digital ''vault'' and protected by multiple layers of encryption.\par \par \uc2 Corey Frayer, director of investor protection at the \uc2 Consumer Federation of America\uc2 , said \uc2 Fidelity\uc2 's concerns about cybersecurity were ''legitimate'' because \uc2 Pontera\uc2  had access to its accounts without a software gateway, known as an API connection. So it's possible \uc2 Pontera\uc2  could gain access to information beyond what the client intends.\par \par \uc2 Pontera\uc2  said that a year ago, it offered to create an API and customize its access to \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  accounts, but that \uc2 Fidelity\uc2  had not responded to its request.\par \par \uc2 Why would someone want an outside adviser to manage a 401(k)?\par \par \uc2 Traditionally, 401(k) and similar defined contribution plans were largely off limits to financial advisers until the employee left the company or retired.\par \par \uc2 The plans first became widely available in the 1980s, and early versions generally offered a limited menu of mutual funds. The addition of target-date funds, intended to automatically become less risky as an investor's retirement approaches, further simplified options.\par \par \uc2 But in recent years, 401(k) plan offerings have become more complex, and some investors need more help to stay on track for retirement, advisers argue. Some plans now offer the option to invest in riskier alternative investments, like cryptocurrency and gold. And menus have expanded. Some plans offer brokerage windows, which let more savvy investors place trades themselves in a much broader selection of investments.\par \par \uc2 Could advisers get access to workplace plans in the past?\par \par \uc2 If people with an employer-based retirement account wanted professional advice from an independent adviser, they would typically send account statements to their advisers. Or they could visit the adviser's office, log into the accounts in person and view the investments together. (\uc2 Fidelity\uc2  says it supports that approach.)\par \par \uc2 Advisers would make recommendations to their clients, who would then have to enact the changes themselves.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that clients often fail to enact the changes, said Kyle Louvar, chief executive of Guided Capital Wealth Management in Houston, where Mr. Havins is a client. ''We'd agree on a plan, but they would oftentimes forget,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 What should savers know about having an adviser manage a 401(k)?\par \par \uc2 Having your adviser manage your 401(k) directly may be more expensive than doing it yourself. \uc2 Pontera\uc2  charges advisers fees of 20 to 30 basis points -- 0.2 to 0.3 percent -- on assets managed through its platform. Mr. Frayer said firms probably passed those costs on to their clients.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Frayer said clients should consider whether it was realistic to expect that their adviser would help them beat the market, if they were also paying higher fees. Even if cybersecurity concerns are resolved, he said, ''I see the fees passed on as a core issue.''\par \par \uc2 Some retirement plans offer automatic rebalancing, said Ben Henry-Moreland, a certified financial planner who writes for Kitces.com, and savers invested in target-date funds don't generally need to rebalance because the funds' investment mix adjusts as they age.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251013elad00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
