\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 It's a Topsy-Turvy Time For New Retail C.E.O.s\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jordyn Holman \par \uc2 1,082 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Taking the helm of a well-known consumer company is the pinnacle of an executive's career, but the current environment has left new leaders at companies like \uc2 Nike\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Peloton little time to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 Settling into the chief executive role is always demanding; there are first-time jitters and outsize tasks. But those who took the role in the past year have also had to deal with shifting policies around tariffs and a potential downturn in consumer spending.\par \par \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2  just hit the one-year mark at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and is trying to improve the experience of the coffee chain's customers and workers. But he's also dealing with higher coffee prices that follow double-digit tariffs on Brazil, a key source of coffee beans around the world.\par \par \uc2 Nike's Elliott Hill is about to hit his first anniversary in a role that entails bringing the sneaker brand back from a yearlong sales decline and getting more people pumped about it in a crowded footwear market. Yet he's forced to devote resources and time to reappraising \uc2 Nike\uc2 's supply chains in countries like Vietnam and China that are being hard hit by tariffs.\par \par \uc2 New leaders at REI, Peloton, Michael's and \uc2 David's Bridal\uc2  may relate.\par \par \uc2 ''I joke that I've gotten the full C.E.O. experience in my first nine months -- just a few years ahead of schedule,'' said \uc2 Peter Stern\uc2 , chief executive of Peloton, who started in January. In addition to leading the company's turnaround -- sales have fallen more than a third after a boom during the pandemic -- Mr. Stern must determine how to price its products, made in places like Taiwan, China and Thailand, where President Trump has placed tariffs.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, every company is trying to grow even as consumers shift their spending habits and become increasingly pessimistic about the economy.\par \par \uc2 ''What's been so challenging is just the uncertainty, the volatility and things changing all the time,'' said \uc2 Mary Beth Laughton\uc2 , chief executive of \uc2 REI\uc2 , referring to tariffs. She started at \uc2 REI\uc2  in February, a couple of days after Mr. Trump signed an executive order that imposed tariffs on China, Mexico and Canada. ''The hardest part of our job right now is trying to anticipate, when things are changing so much.''\par \par \uc2 Still, chief executives must know how to simultaneously satisfy the needs of their boards of directors, employees and customers. And they are well compensated for this, earning more than $1 million in base salary, along with bonuses and stock options valued in the millions. Those taking the top job for the first time must address new problems for which there's often no playbook, and do it while fixing the ones that led the company to find a new leader in the first place.\par \par \uc2 Kelly Cook\uc2 , who started as chief executive at \uc2 David's Bridal\uc2  in April, acknowledged that she had stepped into the role when geopolitical and economic forces were ''reshaping retail.'' But, she said, it's ''exactly the kind of challenge I love.''\par \par \uc2 Consultants say chief executives usually have about 90 days to six months to learn the company, identify its biggest problems and publicly lay out their vision.\par \par \uc2 Oftentimes, executives go on ''listening tours,'' where they meet with employees and customers, to shape their vision for improving the company or revamping their senior leadership team. They could decide to introduce new products, identify new revenue streams or end initiatives by the previous leadership, which could result in closing stores and laying off workers. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , for instance said this week that it would close underperforming stores in North America and lay off about 900 corporate employees, in addition to the 1,100 jobs that the company cut earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Despite the additional challenges, new chief executives aren't graded on a curve.\par \par \uc2 ''You only get one first year,'' said \uc2 Daniel Heaf\uc2 , who became chief executive of \uc2 Bath & Body Works\uc2  in May. Mr. Heaf, a former \uc2 Nike\uc2  executive, recounted the advice he received while going through the interview process. ''What you do in those first 12 months -- whether you realize it or not -- defines your trajectory as a leader.''\par \par \uc2 That may be why turnover is high in the consumer sector: It accounted for a quarter of all chief executive turnover across corporate America, according to a recent report by Crist|Kolder Associates, an executive search firm. The average tenure for a chief executive in consumer businesses was 5.9 years, down slightly from last year. That's among the shortest in American business. At tech firms, for instance, the average was 10 years. In financial services, it was 8.7 years.\par \par \uc2 ''You don't get grace,'' said Christine Greybe, president of leadership consulting at DHR Global, which helps consumer brands identify new leaders. ''There's a very short honeymoon period.''\par \par \uc2 Board directors are using this moment of upheaval in the consumer goods sector to select executives who have never been at the helm, Ms. Greybe said. New chief executives, especially those coming from outside a company, are perceived as having a wealth of new ideas and the ability to shake up operations.\par \par \uc2 ''Historically boards preferred prior corner-office experience, but there's a lot of recycled leaders as a result,'' she said. ''So you're seeing an openness to first-time C.E.O.s.''\par \par \uc2 It comes with its own risks though.\par \par \uc2 This year, Target, \uc2 Yum Brands\uc2  and \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2  named new chief executives; when they start, they, too, will have to contend with outsized issues while trying to please customers and shareholders.\par \par \uc2 ''A clear sense of focus will be critical to navigate the landscape and not get distracted,'' \uc2 Michael Fiddelke\uc2 , who will take over as chief executive of Target in February, said in an emailed statement.\par \par \uc2 A business environment like this might also attract someone who has never reached the top role at a company, like Mr. Fiddelke. They might be more eager to say yes so they can take the opportunity, said \uc2 Kathy Gersch\uc2 , president and founder of the consulting firm Kotter.\par \par \uc2 ''Somebody that's getting an opportunity to be a first-time C.E.O. might be more willing to step into a riskier role than somebody that is a very experienced C.E.O. that may have lots of options in front of them in terms of choices they can make,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251006ela60004e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
