\par\par \uc2 YOUR MONEY ADVISER\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Relying on Pay-Advance Apps for Basic Needs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ann Carrns \par \uc2 1,344 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rising use ''is not a signal of satisfaction,'' an author of a new report says. Rather, heavy users of the apps are under ''financial strain.''\par \par \uc2 Pay-advance apps are marketed as a way to help workers living paycheck to paycheck pay for unexpected expenses, but workers are often using the apps to manage basic expenses like groceries, rent and other needs, a new report found.\par \par \uc2 The tools, consumer advocates say, can carry costs akin to those of traditional payday loans.\par \par \uc2 An analysis of anonymous data found worrisome behavior among users of the apps, including quick increases in the number of advances, advances from multiple apps at the same time and more frequent bank overdraft fees.\par \par \uc2 ''These findings reveal persistent patterns of financial strain that raise serious concerns about the long-term effects of these loans,'' said the report from the \uc2 Center for Responsible Lending\uc2 , a nonprofit consumer advocacy group. The group analyzed data from SaverLife, a nonprofit that promotes saving and sound financial practices among people with low or moderate incomes.\par \par \uc2 The analysis found that heavy users of the apps paid $421, on average, in total loan and overdraft fees over a year, or almost triple the average paid by moderate users.\par \par \uc2 What are pay-advance apps?\par \par \uc2 The apps, also known as ''earned wage access'' or ''on-demand pay'' tools, aim to address the gap between when hourly workers earn their wages and when they are paid. A typical pay cycle is biweekly or monthly, but expenses can crop up before a paycheck arrives. Workers can use the apps to request a portion of their wages early. The amount is then deducted on the user's payday.\par \par \uc2 Pay-advance apps come in two varieties, which work a bit differently. Apps available to the public -- like Brigit, Dave and \uc2 EarnIn\uc2  -- typically link to the user's bank account. Options offered through employers work with a company's payroll system.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Center for Responsible Lending\uc2  studied only workers who used direct-to-consumer apps.\par \par \uc2 Is there a fee to get a paycheck advance?\par \par \uc2 The apps typically charge fees if the worker wants the money delivered immediately -- often a couple of dollars, but sometimes more. Standard delivery may be free, but can take as long as several days, depending on the app. (Employer-based options generally charge lower fees, or sometimes none.)\par \par \uc2 Some apps urge users to pay ''tips'' when taking advances, suggesting the payments are optional. But consumer advocates say it can be difficult in practice to avoid paying them. The \uc2 National Consumer Law Center\uc2  reported this year on how one app peppered a user with 17 prompts for tips and required more than a dozen clicks in the app to avoid paying them.\par \par \uc2 Tenisha James, 48, of Waterbury, Conn., said her job as a sales coordinator for an insurance company pays her every two weeks, so she uses a pay-advance app to help cover expenses between paychecks. While the app charges fees for quick access to funds, she said, it helps her pay her rent on time and is cheaper than double-digit late fees on bills for utilities, cable and other services.\par \par \uc2 ''Why use a credit card when you can use your own money?'' she said.\par \par \uc2 How often can workers take advances?\par \par \uc2 Some apps allow multiple advances per week, or even per day, in amounts ranging from $20 to a few hundred dollars. Typically, a user's maximum withdrawal is set by the app.\par \par \uc2 The researchers tracked workers for a year after their first advance. They found that advances doubled, from an average of two loans per month to four. Nearly three-fourths of users quickly went back for another draw, taking out more than one in a two-week period, the researchers found.\par \par \uc2 Christelle Bamona, a senior researcher at the center and a co-author of the report, said rising use ''is not a signal of satisfaction'' with the product. Rather, she said, the pattern suggests workers are continually trying to make up for a shortfall in their paychecks resulting from repeated loans and fees.\par \par \uc2 Ian P. Moloney, head of policy and regulatory affairs at the American Fintech Council, a group that represents financial technology firms including pay-advance apps, criticized the center's study as mischaracterizing digital tools used by millions of people.\par \par \uc2 ''We urge policymakers to look beyond ideological, ill-informed narratives and engage with the full set of facts before rushing to restrict access to these vital tools,'' he said in an email.\par \par \uc2 Miranda Margowsky, a spokeswoman for the Financial Technology Association, an industry group, said in an email that pay advance tools are ''consumer friendly and easy to use'' and don't affect a user's credit. ''Getting paid once or twice a month doesn't work for many Americans who have to meet routine or emergency expenses,'' she wrote.\par \par \uc2 Are paycheck advances considered loans?\par \par \uc2 That's a debate playing out among government regulators, courts and state legislatures. The financial technology industry insists that earned-wage advances aren't loans, because workers are simply getting quicker access to funds they have already earned.\par \par \uc2 Consumer advocates, and some states, argue that because of the short-term nature of the advances, effective annual interest rates can be high -- triple-digit percentages, similar to the rates on traditional payday loans. The \uc2 Center for Responsible Lending\uc2  analysis found that the average interest rate for advances repaid in seven to 14 days was 383 percent -- close to a typical storefront payday loan.\par \par \uc2 About a dozen states have set rules for the advances. Connecticut this summer, for instance, approved a rule that caps charges at $4 per advance, or $30 per month.\par \par \uc2 New York State sued two pay-advance companies this year, saying that the advances are loans, that their fees are effectively interest and that the interest is illegally high under state consumer laws. The suits are pending.\par \par \uc2 Rules at the federal level are a bit of a muddle. Last year, the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2  moved to define pay advances as a form of credit, which would require providers to disclose the costs. The rule wasn't made final, said Carla Sanchez-Adams, a lawyer with the \uc2 National Consumer Law Center\uc2 . But the agency, under the new Trump administration, has withdrawn other guidance on the advances, leaving the situation unclear.\par \par \uc2 What can workers use the advances for?\par \par \uc2 Workers can use the funds as they wish. While the advances are often marketed as a way to cover unexpected expenses, like a car repair or medical bill, research shows they are often used for basic needs like meals and housing.\par \par \uc2 In a case study of nearly 70 restaurant workers using an employer-based app, published in March by the \uc2 Employee Benefit Research Institute\uc2 , the most common reasons cited for taking a pay advance were food (76 percent) and rent or housing (47 percent).\par \par \uc2 ''The importance of using the funds for essential expenses should be underscored,'' said Bridget Bearden, a research and development strategist at the institute and a co-author of the study. ''There was a perception that it was being used for discretionary expenses.''\par \par \uc2 How can workers use the tools safely?\par \par \uc2 If you want to try a pay advance tool, check first with your employer to see if it offers an in-house program. Workplace apps are generally considered safer, said Leigh Phillips, chief executive of SaverLife, because repayment happens automatically through payroll deduction and fees are often lower or paid by the employer.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Sanchez-Adams advised against ''stacking'' loans, or taking out multiple advances at the same time. That increases the chance that you'll end up with a deficit when your next paycheck arrives. ''It's a debt trap,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bearden said using multiple forms of credit at the same time -- such as combining paycheck advances with credit cards and buy-now-pay-later apps -- was ''a sign of an issue'' and might warrant talking to a financial coach or counselor for advice.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251004ela40003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
