\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 E.P.A. Staff Is Confused About Pay and Furlough Status\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Friedman \par \uc2 515 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 E.P.A. employees said that they were working without pay, but an agency spokeswoman disputed that contention. Union officials said they were ''highly confused.''\par \par \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  employees who expected to be furloughed in the government shutdown were working on Wednesday after receiving a letter late Tuesday informing them that they ''work on activities which currently have funds available.''\par \par \uc2 It was unclear how many of the 15,166 employees at the E.P.A. received the exemption notice, but union officials said that they believed it to have been nearly the entire staff.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for Lee Zeldin, the E.P.A. administrator, said that the agency was operating under its shutdown plan. That plan, however, calls for only 1,734 employees -- about 11 percent of the staff -- to continue working.\par \par \uc2 Federal agencies do have balances of appropriated funds that carry over from the previous funding period. Those funds could be used to stay operational for a short time -- typically a week or two -- after a government shutdown. But Trump administration officials have not acknowledged whether the E.P.A. is being kept open from those funds or from other unspent money from laws passed during the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Several E.P.A. employees said they and their department colleagues did not expect to be paid until after the shutdown ended, but Brigit Hirsch, a spokeswoman for the agency, disputed their contention, saying in an email that ''No \uc2 EPA\uc2  employee is working without pay.'' She pointed to guidelines issued by the White House Office of Personnel Management that said exempted employees working during a shutdown ''will generally continue to be governed by the normal pay, leave, and other civil service rules.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hirsch declined to say how many employees have been exempted, or how long the exemptions would last.\par \par \uc2 ''Continue to work on initiatives as directed by your leadership,'' Aaron Szabo, who leads E.P.A.'s air office, said in a memo to his staff on Wednesday reviewed by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . He advised that employees would be ''notified if there is any change in status.''\par \par \uc2 The lack of transparency, union leaders said, has fed the chaos surrounding the Trump administration's plans for federal workers amid the impasse between Republicans and Democrats over spending.\par \par \uc2 ''Workers at the E.P.A. are highly confused by the behavior of this administration, where they have published shutdown plans that call for furloughs and layoffs but then send out messaging to staff that they are exempted from the shutdown while funds are available,'' said Justin Chen, president of the American Federation of Government Employees Council 238, which represents many E.P.A. employees, in a statement. He added: ''This administration should stop playing games with federal workers.''\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Chellie Pingree\uc2  of Maine, the top Democrat on the appropriations subcommittee overseeing the E.P.A., said in a statement that the agency's lack of clarity was ''deeply unfair to public servants who deserve straight answers about their jobs.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251003ela30003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
