\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Halts Broadcasting Of U.S. Voice To the World\b0\par\par\uc2 By Minho Kim \par \uc2 703 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 During past shutdowns, the federal news agency continued broadcasting to authoritarian countries, as it was considered essential to national security.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration suspended all news broadcasts from Voice of America and furloughed all its journalists on Wednesday after the government shut down, effectively fulfilling its efforts to shutter the agency two days after a judge ordered it to reinstate workers and restore programming.\par \par \uc2 Nearly all the 80 or so remaining employees at the agency, which broadcast news to countries with limited press freedom, are furloughed. Mass furloughs and the suspension of news programming did not occur during past shutdowns, as providing news coverage to authoritarian countries like Russia, China and Iran was considered essential to national security.\par \par \uc2 ''Voice of America broadcasts have been suspended due to a funding cut from the \uc2 United States government\uc2 , which has led to a government shutdown,'' reads a recent notice posted on the website for the news network's Persian-language service. Similar notices appeared on the network's websites for other language services, including Mandarin, Dari and Pashto.\par \par \uc2 But a shutdown preparation document published last year by the news group's oversight agency, the U.S. Agency for Global Media, listed about 650 Voice of America employees and journalists as essential to ''perform activities expressly authorized by law.''\par \par \uc2 A similar document published last month, however, does not designate Voice of America as essential, although it mentions the Office of Cuba Broadcasting, another federal agency that provides news coverage to Cubans in Spanish, as ''foreign relations activities essential to the national security.''\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake, a fierce Trump ally and the acting chief executive at the oversight agency, said her agency was ''following all applicable law and related guidance'' from the \uc2 White House's Office of Management and Budget\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But she did not say what had prompted her agency to make such drastic changes to the number of employees deemed essential to national security, or why broadcasting to Cuba was treated differently than to other U.S. adversaries.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has threatened to leverage the shutdown to fire or lay off more federal workers and slash programs that he disfavors. On Tuesday, he threatened to fire ''a lot'' of federal workers during the shutdown, despite legal challenges from workers' unions to the president's authority to do so.\par \par \uc2 The decision to pause broadcasting and furlough all journalists arrived two days after a Reagan-appointed federal judge mandated that the government rescind layoff notices sent to more than 500 Voice of America employees. On Wednesday, the agency furloughed those and about 80 more people.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, that judge, Royce C. Lamberth of the U.S. District Court for the District of Columbia, threatened to hold Trump officials in contempt for failing to restore the volume of news programming at Voice of America to roughly what it had been before March, as he had ordered.\par \par \uc2 Voice of America had been airing news broadcasts to 360 million people in 49 languages every week until mid-March, when Mr. Trump effectively ordered the agency's dismantling. Since then, it provided about an hourlong news service every day in each of four languages: Persian, spoken in Iran and neighboring countries; Mandarin; and the two main languages spoken in Afghanistan, Dari and Pashto.\par \par \uc2 Judge Lamberth had for weeks tried to obtain adequate information that would demonstrate the Trump administration's compliance with his ruling from April, when he ordered a restoration of Voice of America's news coverage so that it could ''serve as a consistently reliable and authoritative source of news.''\par \par \uc2 ''The defendants' obfuscation of this court's requests for information,'' he wrote on Monday, ''has wasted precious judicial time and resources and readily support contempt proceedings.''\par \par \uc2 The three plaintiffs representing Voice of America employees in a lawsuit against the Trump administration, Kate Neeper, Jessica Jerreat and Patsy Widakuswara, said in a joint statement that the shuttering of the news group was ''heartbreaking.''\par \par \uc2 ''We've always served our audiences and continued performing our vital national security role while explaining U.S. policy during past shutdowns,'' they said. ''What V.O.A. once was is now further diminished.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251003ela300032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
