\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 White House Withdraws Pick to Lead Labor Data Agency Without Explanation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tony Romm, Ben Casselman and Lydia DePillis \par \uc2 749 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 E.J. Antoni, a conservative economist, had faced bipartisan criticism as President Trump's nominee to lead the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The White House has withdrawn the nomination of E.J. Antoni, a conservative economist, to serve as the leader of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , the federal agency whose previous director was fired by President Trump after issuing an unfavorable jobs report.\par \par \uc2 A White House official confirmed the withdrawal late Tuesday, but offered no reason for the sudden change, saying only that Mr. Antoni was a ''brilliant economist'' and that Mr. Trump would announce a new nominee soon.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump tapped Mr. Antoni in August, shortly after ousting Erika McEntarfer, the previous B.L.S. commissioner, over unsubstantiated claims that the bureau had ''rigged'' data. Mr. Trump had seized on a report that month showing that hiring had been weaker than reported over the summer -- a large but otherwise normal revision that the president nonetheless claimed as evidence of bias at the agency.\par \par \uc2 From the start, Mr. Antoni had been a divisive pick. A doctor in economics, he had no prior experience working in the government, having spent most of his career as a highly partisan economic analyst and commentator. Most past commissioners, by contrast, have been statistical experts with little public profile before leading the agency.\par \par \uc2 ''Typically, frankly, you're getting a data nerd,'' said Erica L. Groshen, who ran the agency under President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Antoni had shared Mr. Trump's criticisms of B.L.S. and frequently attacked its reports and methodologies. That troubled economists and other experts from across the political spectrum, who further decried Mr. Antoni's lack of management experience and his history of distorting economic statistics to support his political arguments.\par \par \uc2 Aaron Sojourner, a labor economist who serves on the steering committee of the Friends of B.L.S., an advocacy organization, said he was pleased that the administration was withdrawing Mr. Antoni's nomination.\par \par \uc2 ''This was an underqualified pick, and it's good that the process worked,'' Mr. Sojourner said. He added that he hoped the administration would instead choose someone with more experience ''who can be a champion for the agency and its mission.''\par \par \uc2 CNN earlier reported Mr. Antoni's withdrawal. Mr. Antoni did not immediately respond to a request for comment. Kevin Roberts, the president of \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , said in a social media post that Mr. Antoni would remain as the group's chief economist.\par \par \uc2 It was unclear whom Mr. Trump planned to nominate instead for a post that requires Senate confirmation. It is currently led on an interim basis by William J. Wiatrowski, the deputy commissioner, who has served as acting head twice before.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wiatrowski is soon to be the only employee working at the agency, after Republicans and Democrats in Congress failed to agree on a spending proposal to avoid a government shutdown. The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  will suspend all operations when federal funding lapses at 12:01 a.m. on Wednesday. The entire staff other than Mr. Wiatrowski will be furloughed.\par \par \uc2 The next commissioner will take over the bureau at a difficult moment. Even before Mr. Trump returned to office, the agency faced longstanding challenges because of falling survey response rates and years of shrinking budgets. High rates of staff attrition and a freeze on filling vacant roles have made those problems even worse under Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 Ms. McEntarfer pointed to those challenges earlier this month, in her first public comments since her ouster, as she warned against the politicization of economic data.\par \par \uc2 ''Markets have to trust the data are not manipulated,'' Ms. McEntarfer said. ''Firing your chief statisticians for releasing data you do not like -- it has serious economic consequences. The list of countries that have gone down this route, it's not a good list.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's sudden firing of Ms. McEntarfer also means the next commissioner will almost certainly face skepticism from inside and outside the agency. That will make the job more difficult, said William Beach, who ran the agency during the first Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a much bigger job today than it was on July 31,'' the day before Ms. McEntarfer was fired, he said. ''The next person has to establish him or herself as a barrier to meddling by political forces both in Congress and the White House.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251002ela20004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
