\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Shutdown's Effect on Federal Services and Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elena Shao and Lazaro Gamio \par \uc2 1,506 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The government shutdown that began Wednesday morning suspended the work of hundreds of thousands of employees, disrupting a wide range of federal programs. Many more employees are required to report to work without pay until funding is restored.\par \par \uc2 The impacts vary from department to department. Some services and programs will continue mostly uninterrupted if they are self-funded or considered ''mandatory,'' such as the Postal Service and Social Security benefits. Departments also designated some employees, such as federal law enforcement officers, active-duty troops and air traffic controllers, as ''essential.'' Many will work without pay while the shutdown persists.\par \par \uc2 Some shutdowns have ended after a few days, but this impasse is particularly contentious. President Trump on Tuesday doubled down on a threat to fire federal employees if funding lapses, and suggested he could leverage the shutdown to enact measures that are ''bad'' for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Here is a look at some of the activities and programs that will continue and some that have paused, at least for now, based on plans that federal agencies made public in recent days.\par \par \uc2 Agriculture\par \par \uc2 85,907 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP), also known as food stamps, and other nutrition programs for women and children\par \par \uc2 Wildfire response\par \par \uc2 Inspections of meat, poultry and egg products\par \par \uc2 Animal and plant health programs related to diseases like bird flu\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 Access to remote recreational sites\par \par \uc2 Non-essential research and data collection\par \par \uc2 Hazardous fuel treatments, including prescribed fires\par \par \uc2 Note: Date of total employee count is not known.\par \par \uc2 Commerce\par \par \uc2 42,984 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Weather forecasts and warnings to the public of hazardous weather conditions\par \par \uc2 Various maritime activities, including fishery monitoring, water level monitoring for ships entering U.S. ports and updates to nautical charts\par \par \uc2 Patent processing and approval, as long as reserve funds are available\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 Most activities at the Census Bureau, like survey collection or the production of monthly reports\par \par \uc2 Most research activities across the department\par \par \uc2 Note: Date of total employee count is not known.\par \par \uc2 Defense (civilian work force)\par \par \uc2 741,477 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Military operations, but troops will work without pay during the shutdown\par \par \uc2 Recruiting and training operations\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 New defense contracts\par \par \uc2 Note: Date of total employee count is not known.\par \par \uc2 Education\par \par \uc2 2,447 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Disbursement of student aid, such as Federal Pell Grants and Federal Direct Student Loans (and loan borrowers are still required to make payments on outstanding debt)\par \par \uc2 Title I and Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) grants funding\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 New grantmaking activities (though grantees will still be able to access previously awarded funds)\par \par \uc2 Review and investigations of complaints by the \uc2 Office for Civil Rights\par \par \uc2 Note: Total employee count is as of Sept. 20. The total number of employees who must work without pay could not be determined.\par \par \uc2 Energy\par \par \uc2 13,812 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Maintenance, development and safeguarding of nuclear weapons\par \par \uc2 Directing power to utilities\par \par \uc2 Note: Total employee count is as of Oct. 1.\par \par \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\par \par \uc2 15,166 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Emergency and disaster assistance\par \par \uc2 Protection of E.P.A. land, buildings and equipment\par \par \uc2 Preservation of ongoing experiments\par \par \uc2 Cleanup of Superfund sites that pose ''an imminent threat to human life''\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 Issuance of new permits, guidance, regulations or grants\par \par \uc2 Cleanup of Superfund sites where the E.P.A. has determined there is ''no imminent threat to human health and property''\par \par \uc2 Note: Date of total employee count is not known.\par \par \uc2 Health and Human Services\par \par \uc2 79,717 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Medicare benefits (however, the \uc2 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services\uc2  may not be able to issue replacement cards)\par \par \uc2 Preservation of ongoing experiments at the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  (N.I.H.)\par \par \uc2 Operations at the N.I.H. biomedical research hospital\par \par \uc2 Outbreak response\par \par \uc2 The World Trade Center Health Program and the Vaccines for Children program under the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  (\uc2 C.D.C.\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Review of some drug and medical devices by the Food and Drug Administration (F.D.A.) and investigations and inspections of imports\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 Routine safety surveillance of products including compounded obesity medications\par \par \uc2 Efforts to eliminate problematic food chemicals and replace them with safer alternatives\par \par \uc2 Routine inspections of food and medication facilities\par \par \uc2 Issuance of new N.I.H. grants and basic research conducted by N.I.H. scientists\par \par \uc2 Admittance of new patients at the N.I.H. hospital\par \par \uc2 C.D.C.\uc2  guidance to state and local health departments on public health matters, including opioid overdose prevention and diabetes prevention\par \par \uc2 F.D.A. approval of some new drug and medical device applications\par \par \uc2 Note: Total employee count is as of July 31.\par \par \uc2 Homeland Security\par \par \uc2 271,927 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Work from immigration, border and customs agents, including most of the U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement's 20,000 employees\par \par \uc2 FEMA disaster response\par \par \uc2 Customs enforcement on imports, including the collection of tariffs\par \par \uc2 Citizenship and green card applications, although delays could occur\par \par \uc2 Duties of \uc2 Transportation Security Administration\uc2  airport screeners (who will continue to work without pay)\par \par \uc2 Note: Total employee count is as of May 31.\par \par \uc2 Housing and Urban Development\par \par \uc2 6,105 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Grant programs providing emergency housing for the homeless\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 Review of housing discrimination complaints\par \par \uc2 Note: Date of total employee count is not known.\par \par \uc2 Interior\par \par \uc2 58,619 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Access to some national parks, but some services may be unavailable\par \par \uc2 Inspections of oil and gas and logging operations\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 Public access to some wildlife refuges\par \par \uc2 Most \uc2 U.S. Geological Survey\uc2  data collection and dissemination, including satellite and water quality data\par \par \uc2 Note: Date of total employee count is not known.\par \par \uc2 Justice\par \par \uc2 115,131 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Criminal litigation\par \par \uc2 Work of the \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  and agents at the \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2 , the \uc2 Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives\uc2  field divisions and the \uc2 Drug Enforcement Administration\par \par \uc2 Maintenance of federal prisons, including medical care of inmates\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 Most civil litigation\par \par \uc2 Most training of state and local officers\par \par \uc2 Note: Total employee count is as of May 31.\par \par \uc2 Labor\par \par \uc2 12,916 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Mandated inspections and investigations of mines\par \par \uc2 Unemployment insurance benefits\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 Data releases from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , which produces monthly jobs data\par \par \uc2 Inspections of situations that are not considered to be posing ''imminent danger,'' as authorized by the Occupational Safety and Health Act\par \par \uc2 Note: Date of total employee count is not known. Figures do not reflect 17 presidential appointees who are not subject to furlough.\par \par \uc2 Small Business Administration\par \par \uc2 6,201 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Approval and disbursement of disaster loans\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 Processing of loan applications by the S.B.A.'s 7(a) and 504 loan guarantee programs, which back bank loans totaling more than $44 billion a year to tens of thousands of small companies\par \par \uc2 Note: Total employee count is as of September.\par \par \uc2 Social Security Administration\par \par \uc2 51,825 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Social Security retirement and disability payments\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 Benefit verifications\par \par \uc2 Note: Date of total employee count is not known.\par \par \uc2 State\par \par \uc2 26,995 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Visa and passport processing\par \par \uc2 U.S. embassies and consulates abroad\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 Most new grants and contracts\par \par \uc2 Note: Date of total employee count is not known. The total number of employees who must work without pay could not be determined.\par \par \uc2 Transportation\par \par \uc2 53,717 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Air traffic controllers (who will work without pay)\par \par \uc2 Note: Date of total employee count is not known.\par \par \uc2 Treasury\par \par \uc2 81,165 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Duties of the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2 , for at least the first five business days of the shutdown\par \par \uc2 Economic and market recommendations and updates to the President\par \par \uc2 Activities that will cease\par \par \uc2 Issuance of industry regulations and guidance\par \par \uc2 Note: Date of total employee count is not known.\par \par \uc2 Veterans Affairs\par \par \uc2 461,499 employees\par \par \uc2 Activities that will continue\par \par \uc2 Veteran medical care, homelessness programs and the Veterans Crisis Line\par \par \uc2 Pension, housing and other benefits for veterans\par \par \uc2 Note: Total employee count is as of March.\par \par \uc2 Eileen Sullivan, Stacy Cowley and Madeleine Ngo contributed reporting. Methodology: Not all agencies explicitly stated the number of employees who must work and those who would be furloughed. In those cases, the numbers were calculated from additional information included in the plans, and may include some duplicate counts. Data does not necessarily reflect the most up-to-date employment numbers for an agency's total work force; some agencies provided numbers based on personnel data from as early as March 2025. Data is based on publicly available contingency plans from the departments of Agriculture, Commerce, Defense, Education, Energy, Health and Human Services, Homeland Security, Housing and Urban Development, Interior, Justice, Labor, Transportation, Veterans Affairs, \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 , Office of Personnel Management, \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2 , \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2  and Treasury.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251002ela20003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
