\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Shutdown Could Blind Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Casselman \par \uc2 1,047 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ''Flying blind amidst heavy fog is a dangerous proposition,'' one economist said about the risks of a lapse in official statistics.\par \par \uc2 The monthly jobs report on Friday is expected to provide crucial evidence to economists, policymakers and investors who are trying to determine whether a slowdown in hiring over the summer continued into the fall, and whether deeper cracks are forming in the foundation of the U.S. labor market.\par \par \uc2 If, that is, there is a jobs report on Friday at all.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , which produces the monthly jobs data, will ''suspend all operations'' if Congress and the president fail to reach a deal to extend funding for the federal government before a Tuesday midnight deadline, according to a contingency plan released by the Department of Labor. Data releases scheduled during a government shutdown ''will not be released,'' and data collection for future reports will cease, the memo said.\par \par \uc2 That means a shutdown could delay not only the jobs report on Friday but also other data, including key inflation figures that were set to come out in mid-October. Reports from other agencies, including the Census Bureau and Bureau of Economic Analysis -- which are part of the Commerce Department -- would also be affected.\par \par \uc2 The potential lapse in data comes at a particularly critical moment for economists and policymakers, who have been struggling to make sense of conflicting signals about the health of the labor market and the broader economy. Job growth has slowed significantly in recent months, but the unemployment rate has remained low. Measures of consumer spending and some other economic activity have likewise stayed relatively strong.\par \par \uc2 ''We are currently seeing an economy that may or may not be at a turning point, particularly for the labor market,'' said \uc2 Martha Gimbel\uc2 , who worked at the \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  during the Biden administration and is now executive director of the Budget Lab at Yale. Such turning points are always difficult to recognize in real time, she said, which makes it even more important to have as much high-quality data as possible.\par \par \uc2 ''Less data is not going to make understanding the economy at such a pivotal time easier,'' Ms. Gimbel said.\par \par \uc2 An extended government shutdown would add yet another complication for officials at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , who are trying to weigh the risks of a weakening labor market against the threat posed by stubborn inflation. Without reliable data, the chances are greater that they will make an error in one direction or the other, allowing either unemployment or inflation to worsen.\par \par \uc2 ''Flying blind amidst heavy fog is a dangerous proposition,'' said \uc2 Gregory Daco\uc2 , chief economist at the consulting firm \uc2 EY\uc2 -Parthenon.\par \par \uc2 How long that fog persists depends on the length of any shutdown.\par \par \uc2 A brief lapse in funding might have only a limited impact. Data collection for the September jobs report has already been completed, and the report itself -- which includes dozens of tables alongside charts and other materials -- will be mostly finished by the time federal funding expires at 12:01 a.m. on Wednesday. As a result, the government should be able to release the report quickly once the government reopens, said William Beach, who was commissioner of the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  from 2019 to 2023.\par \par \uc2 Data on consumer prices for September has also been collected, meaning that the next Consumer Price Index report, scheduled for Oct. 15, should be ready quickly once funding is restored.\par \par \uc2 But if a shutdown lasts more than a couple of weeks, it could disrupt the process of collecting data for future reports, which could have more lasting consequences. Labor force statistics, for example, are based on a survey conducted in the middle of each month. If the survey is delayed, people could struggle to recall what they were doing weeks earlier, which could make the data less reliable.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Labor, in its planning document, warned that ''a reduction in quality of data collected might impact the quality of future estimates produced.''\par \par \uc2 This would not be the first time that a government shutdown disrupted the nation's economic data. A 16-day lapse in funding in 2013 delayed dozens of releases, including the September jobs report. A longer but less extensive shutdown during President Trump's first term spared the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  but held up reports from the Commerce Department, including data on gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 This disruption, however, would come at a particularly tumultuous period for the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . In August, Mr. Trump fired the head of the agency following an unexpectedly weak jobs report, then nominated E.J. Antoni, a conservative economist, to replace her. Mr. Antoni is still awaiting Senate confirmation.\par \par \uc2 The agency has also been grappling with staffing shortages, which have forced it to scale back data collection in some areas. The bureau recently posted job openings for more than two dozen part-time employees to help fill those gaps, but a government shutdown could delay the hiring process.\par \par \uc2 ''It's another obstacle being thrown in their path,'' said \uc2 Erica Groshen\uc2 , who led the statistics agency the last time it had to shut down because of a lapse in funding in 2013.\par \par \uc2 In the absence of most government data, economists and policymakers will be looking to alternative sources. The Fed, which does not rely on federal funding, will continue to produce data on manufacturing output, consumer credit and other topics. Private companies such as Indeed, the career website, and ADP, the payroll processor, also release data on job openings, hiring and wages.\par \par \uc2 But none of those private sources are as comprehensive or authoritative as the official government statistics -- and many of them are based on government data to some degree. No private sources can fully replace data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  and other agencies, said Kathy Jones, chief fixed-income strategist at \uc2 Charles Schwab\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''The B.L.S. figures are the gold standard for evaluating trends in the labor market,'' she said. ''So setting policy without those numbers, especially at a time when the Fed is very much focused on the labor market, would be difficult.''\par \par \uc2 Tony Romm and Colby Smith contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Tony Romm and Colby Smith contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251001ela10004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
