\par\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 In Venice, Strike Threats Greet New Music Director\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elisabetta Povoledo \par \uc2 1,103 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers at La Fenice say Beatrice Venezi, a favorite of Prime Minister \uc2 Giorgia Meloni\uc2  of Italy, lacks the experience to be the storied company's music director.\par \par \uc2 La Fenice Theater in Venice is one of Italy's most famous opera venues. Classic works by Bellini, Rossini and Verdi had their premieres there, and it regularly hosts some of the world's top conductors.\par \par \uc2 When theater officials announced last week that La Fenice's new music director would be the conductor Beatrice Venezi, some members of the company were outraged, saying she hasn't had enough experience. Venezi had not conducted at La Fenice before, save for a brief, promotional event, and many staff members only knew her from TV appearances and from an ad for a hair product.\par \par \uc2 La Fenice workers are now threatening to strike.\par \par \uc2 In addition to what some critics call a lack of experience, other critics have said that Venezi, 35, is close to Prime Minister \uc2 Giorgia Meloni\uc2  and her hard-right Brothers of Italy Party, and suggested that may have played a role.\par \par \uc2 Venezi has never hidden that her political sympathies lie with the right. In a 2023 interview in the newspaper La Stampa, she said she identified with the right's conservative values, but added that she was offended when people called her a ''fascistella'' (little Fascist) because her father, Gabriele Venezi, had held a regional leadership role in Forza Nuova, another hard-right party.\par \par \uc2 Meloni has praised Venezi on social media as ''a courageous and talented artist who refuses to bow to the dictatorship of thought and language'' of ''ideological fanatics.'' Shortly after Meloni came to power, Venezi was named as the government's official councilor for music.\par \par \uc2 ''Venezi is not a famous conductor who happens to be right wing,'' said Alberto Mattioli, a classical music critic. ''She's famous because she is right wing.''\par \par \uc2 Since Meloni became prime minister in 2022, some observers have accused her government of trying to put a conservative imprint on Italian cultural institutions. A pattern has played out, they say, in the decision to favor Italians at the helm of national museums and in the choice of exhibitions to showcase, as well as in the world of opera companies, where the government has made clear it favors homegrown talent over foreigners.\par \par \uc2 Other opera companies expressed their support for La Fenice workers' resisting Venezi's appointment. Staff members at La Scala, the Milan opera house, put out a statement last week in which they deplored that Venezi had been selected and ''imposed from above.''\par \par \uc2 Marco Trentin, a cellist and union representative at La Fenice, said that while the theater's general manager has the right to select the music director, ''it has been customary'' to consult the orchestra, choir and other company members.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the theater's general manager, Nicola Colabianchi, had assured the staff that Venezi was only one of several people he was considering, and had said that a decision would only be made in a year's time. A few days later, the company announced that Venezi had been selected.\par \par \uc2 ''The union felt betrayed,'' Trentin said.\par \par \uc2 Venezi did not respond to interview requests. In contrast to what some of her critics say, she is hardly a debutante. Her carefully curated social media profiles track her glamorous presence in theaters around the world, most recently in Bangkok where she conducted alongside Pl\u225\'00\'E1cido Domingo at an international festival.\par \par \uc2 She made a 2018 Forbes Italia list as one of 100 influential Italians under 30 and she has hosted television programs on Italy's national broadcaster RAI. Earlier this year, the program ''Viva Puccini,'' which sought to broaden the composer's appeal to a younger audience, was slammed by a noted TV critic as ''kitsch,'' focusing more on Venezi than on Puccini. For several years, she has appeared in a series of commercials for an Italian hair products company, often energetically tossing her thick mane of hair, a trademark of her conducting style.\par \par \uc2 Mattioli, the music critic, acknowledged that Venezi was ''a professional,'' but he noted in an interview that she had never worked at top-tier institutions in ''Vienna, Munich, Berlin, Paris, London or at La Scala.'' Instead, she had conducted mostly at ''very modest theaters,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Among Venezi's supporters, the opera expert Enrico Stinchelli pointed out that she was a regular at the Teatro Col\u243\'00\'F3n in Buenos Aires, and had extensive experience at smaller Italian houses, a more than laudable curriculum for someone her age. Having seen her work, he had found her to be ''prepared and serious.''\par \par \uc2 ''People can like or not like her style, that's a personal taste,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In a letter, Colabianchi apologized to La Fenice's workers for failing to discuss her appointment with the staff. In a telephone interview, he said he had thought he could avoid the polemics that began brewing in the media when her name first emerged as a possible musical director by making a quick decision. ''It was unimaginable that there would be such widespread opposition,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 But he defended his choice, saying he had worked with Venezi when he was general manager of the opera house in Cagliari, Italy, and had appreciated her professionalism. He also said that Venezi's gender, youth and media notoriety would be ''value added'' for the theater in terms of ''international visibility and its message of generational change and innovation.''\par \par \uc2 ''I am certain of what I am doing, because we live in an era where image and communication is important and we have to reach out to a new public,'' he said. He could have selected an older, more established conductor, he said, ''but instead I thought of sending a sign of renewal.''\par \par \uc2 On her social media accounts, Venezi said that she was ''deeply moved and grateful'' to have been selected and that she looked forward to ''beginning this human, artistic and musical dialogue with the theater, the city of Venice, and its audience.''\par \par \uc2 Trentin, of La Fenice trade union, said he hoped Colabianchi would be open to reassessing his decision ''to find a solution.'' Last Saturday, members of the company distributed leaflets outside La Fenice before a concert, and inside the theater, a member of the orchestra read a statement calling for the ''immediate revocation'' of Venezi's appointment. The speech was met with rapturous applause and some standing ovations.\par \par \uc2 For now, before a strike, the protests would take different forms, Trentin said. ''Let's see where it gets us,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251001ela10003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
