\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Former Federal Workers Lament a Nation's Losses\b0\par\par\uc2 By Minho Kim, Aishvarya Kavi and Jared Soares \par \uc2 2,678 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In interviews, two dozen former federal workers from across the country shared what they left behind and what they face next.\par \par \uc2 One in eight federal workers, or some 300,000, will have left the government by the end of December. It's possible even more could be pushed out if the government shuts down next week.\par \par \uc2 Many chose to resign amid concerns of widespread cuts, and will soon receive their last paycheck. Others were fired because they were new to the job, while a variety of positions were eliminated.\par \par \uc2 Some of the workers said that the Trump administration's decisions halted efforts to combat wildfires, stymied public health programs that help lower cancer rates and stopped the creation of free, public tax filing software.\par \par \uc2 ''It's just a huge drain,'' said Cynthia Vinson, who left the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  after 26 years. ''All of the work that I have done in my career is being flushed down the toilet.''\par \par \uc2 The administration has defended its cuts as an effort to reduce spending and boost efficiency.\par \par \uc2 ''These reforms are about making government leaner, more accountable and more effective for taxpayers,'' Scott Kupor, the director of the Office of Personnel Management, said in a statement. ''Work force changes are always difficult.''\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  photographed and interviewed two dozen former federal workers from across the country to get a sense of how they are doing.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Black\par \par \uc2 Idaho Falls, Idaho\par \par \uc2 Department of Energy\par \par \uc2 Took deferred resignation option\par \par \uc2 It was a real missed opportunity for the Trump administration and for DOGE.\par \par \uc2 At first, Mr. Black was cautiously optimistic about the \uc2 Department of Government Efficiency\uc2 , a group formed by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to detect waste, fraud and abuse in the government. That had been Mr. Black's job for the last 33 years.\par \par \uc2 As the chief adviser to the Energy Department's inspector general, he had designed systems that could verify the accuracy of various federal loans and grants amounting to hundreds of billions of dollars. He expected DOGE to take full advantage of his expertise.\par \par \uc2 They didn't. Instead, Mr. Black's boss, Teri Donaldson, was one of the 17 inspectors general Mr. Trump fired a few days after his inauguration. Mr. Black thought it was a mistake. When he realized it wasn't, he decided to leave the only workplace he'd ever known. He is now retired.\par \par \uc2 Von Robertson\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 General Services Administration\par \par \uc2 Laid off\par \par \uc2 They abolished the entire division. It did not abolish the work. They fired people without getting rid of the work.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Robertson ensured that federal agencies were not overcharged for transportation services they received from private companies. She spent more than 30 years in the government and now runs her own career coaching business.\par \par \uc2 Charlotte Colvin\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 U.S. Agency for International Development\par \par \uc2 Contract terminated\par \par \uc2 It feels almost career-ending, because \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\uc2  was the main donor supporting tuberculosis care around the world. I don't really know what I'm going to do next.\par \par \uc2 For 12 years, Dr. Colvin managed all programs that treated tuberculosis in children as a contractor at the agency. Eighteen countries relied on the United States for nearly all of their funding. Dr. Colvin is still without a job.\par \par \uc2 Joseph Edwards\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles\par \par \uc2 AmeriCorps\par \par \uc2 Laid off\par \par \uc2 The return on investment of tax dollars to \uc2 AmeriCorps\uc2  is literally tenfold. Every $1 invested in \uc2 AmeriCorps\uc2 , the community would get $10 in return.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Edwards kept the agency's grant-making operation running. The job is essential to the group's mission of supporting impoverished American communities, as its volunteer programs operate via grants awarded to states, nonprofits and local governments. He is looking for his next job.\par \par \uc2 Merici Vinton\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 U.S. Digital Service\par \par \uc2 Resigned, no additional compensation\par \par \uc2 DOGE had no interest in building a modern product team. But that's what they said they were going to do, right? To have all these private sector start-up people come into government to make it better.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Vinton's team created a free, public tax filing software for the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2 , but when DOGE took over, it was sidelined. Ms. Vinton is now a research fellow, sharing lessons from developing this software.\par \par \uc2 Quay Crowner\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 Department of Education\par \par \uc2 Laid off\par \par \uc2 There's nothing political about the job that I do or have done. We all serve an administration and the administration's priorities, and we do it with integrity.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Crowner was responsible for helping Native American tribes get access to federal student loans and other aid, as many students from tribal communities often struggle to obtain federal resources.\par \par \uc2 Across the federal government, the Trump administration has canceled programs focused on addressing systemic inequities, including Ms. Crowner's. The Supreme Court has also ruled that Mr. Trump's executive order to dismantle the Education Department can move forward.\par \par \uc2 After 32 years of working for the federal government, Ms. Crowner isn't sure what's next. She is focusing on her health, having suffered a traumatic brain injury last year.\par \par \uc2 Edgar Munoz\par \par \uc2 Chicago\par \par \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\par \par \uc2 Laid off\par \par \uc2 We're not an advocacy group for immigration. We are an advocacy group for having an efficient system.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Munoz's job was to fix bottlenecks in the immigration system.\par \par \uc2 Having moved to the United States from Mexico as a teenager, Mr. Munoz wanted to help those who, like his family, had navigated America's complex immigration system.\par \par \uc2 The ombudsman office he worked for was responsible for reviewing immigration petitions that were possibly denied in error. It also worked to streamline the overall process, in particular applicants' ability to renew paperwork that allowed them to legally work in the United States, as delays in this process sometimes cost people their jobs.\par \par \uc2 But his oversight office, along with two other watchdog groups at the department, have been shuttered. Mr. Munoz worries that immigrants who are trying to enter the country legally now have very little recourse.\par \par \uc2 Julianne P. Weis\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 U.S.A.I.D.\par \par \uc2 Laid off\par \par \uc2 We're going to see steep increases in maternal, infant and child deaths that are completely preventable.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Weis oversaw funding for women's reproductive health care in Niger. Now, she has helped found an advocacy group called Aid on the Hill that pushes for the restoration of U.S. foreign assistance programs. She is looking for her next job.\par \par \uc2 Stetson Kastengren\par \par \uc2 Redding, Calif.\par \par \uc2 Bureau of Indian Affairs\par \par \uc2 Fired, probationary employee\par \par \uc2 There's no way to ''privatize or outsource'' the work this agency does for tribal nations.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kastengren's office helped Native Americans navigate property rights and federal benefits at the \uc2 Bureau of Indian Affairs\uc2 . He had just been promoted in December. He has since moved to Rapid City, S.D., where he is a cardiac technician.\par \par \uc2 Evan Thaler\par \par \uc2 Boise, Idaho\par \par \uc2 U.S. Geological Survey\par \par \uc2 Fired, probationary employee\par \par \uc2 I don't begrudge anyone that went back, but I think taking the job back is kind of like letting the playground bully get away with their behavior.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Thaler mapped out where streams run dry, critical information for farmers and foresters in the area. He was the only probationary employee in his office who didn't accept an offer to return to his job. He is now in a one-year research position at \uc2 Oregon State University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Brittany Myatt\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 Department of Education\par \par \uc2 Laid off\par \par \uc2 You didn't need a lawyer. You didn't have to pay a bunch of attorneys fees just to be heard.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Myatt oversaw cases in which students had experienced discrimination because of their disability, race or gender and worked with them to find solutions with the schools. Now she will represent students directly in her own law firm.\par \par \uc2 Jessica Lawrence\par \par \uc2 Portland, Ore.\par \par \uc2 Department of Veterans Affairs\par \par \uc2 Resigned, no additional compensation\par \par \uc2 My colleagues are going to have less time to focus on some of our patients' needs.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lawrence was a licensed social worker responsible for helping hospitalized veterans with substance abuse and medical conditions and for creating treatment plans. Her role was deemed ''mission critical'' and was exempted from layoffs and buyout offers.\par \par \uc2 But she still decided to leave, concerned for her future job security amid chaotic cuts across the government.\par \par \uc2 Her last few weeks on the job gave her confidence that she made the right call. Ms. Lawrence said her coworkers lacked the private space required to ensure that sensitive patient information remained secret. She could overhear details on patients' sexual orientations, gender identities, and substance abuse and housing situations.\par \par \uc2 After she resigned in May, her hospital did not hire a replacement, she said, and her patients were reassigned to other social workers who were already stretched thin. Ms. Lawrence is now practicing privately as a therapist.\par \par \uc2 Victor Udoewa\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\par \par \uc2 Fired\par \par \uc2 That idea of 'move fast and break things,' maybe you can do that in a video game that doesn't matter. But when you get to the benefits that people receive, people's lives are affected. People die.\par \par \uc2 When Covid first started spreading, the \uc2 C.D.C.\uc2  had a tech problem. The agency had trouble compiling numbers for confirmed cases and deaths because of an outdated data system.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Udoewa was recruited from \uc2 NASA\uc2  to fix it. He later helped the \uc2 C.D.C.\uc2  adopt artificial intelligence to improve its health outcomes.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Udoewa spent 11 years at the federal government as a technology designer and said that bringing new tech into the government requires a deep understanding of how it works at every level from hiring to budgeting and procurement policy. But he said that the DOGE team did not try to understand how the government worked before radically disrupting it.\par \par \uc2 He now works as a consultant for Bloom Works, a digital services firm for state and local governments.\par \par \uc2 Soledad Ivaldi\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 National Institutes of Health\par \par \uc2 Fired, probationary employee\par \par \uc2 Pharmacy and biotech companies don't have the time and the money to invest in research for such a long time.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ivaldi worked to make N.I.H.-funded research on Alzheimer's and dementia more accessible to the wider scientific community. A molecular biologist by training, she now works at an elementary school.\par \par \uc2 John Andrews\par \par \uc2 Norfolk, Va.\par \par \uc2 Department of Defense\par \par \uc2 Took deferred resignation option\par \par \uc2 No planning, no consideration, no nothing. It's not anything that I've ever seen in my life before.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Andrews, who served in the Navy for 30 years as an officer and pilot, spent the last few years of his career training civilian sailors in the Navy. He is now retired.\par \par \uc2 Bobby Boyd\par \par \uc2 Kalamazoo, Mich.\par \par \uc2 Department of Energy\par \par \uc2 Took deferred resignation option\par \par \uc2 Low-income Black and brown communities ''don't reach out'' to the federal government ''because they don't know'' about those opportunities.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Boyd helped underrepresented communities get federal grants, but his office of community engagement no longer exists. He is now working in local government, supporting investment and business opportunities for Kalamazoo.\par \par \uc2 Jessica Henry\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 N.I.H.\par \par \uc2 Laid off\par \par \uc2 We did so much with a very small budget.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Henry's job was to distill complex health information, like how diabetes affects oral health, for the general public. She has paused her job search to focus on her small business but plans to look again in the new year.\par \par \uc2 Melanie Maino-Vieytes\par \par \uc2 Atlanta\par \par \uc2 C.D.C.\par \par \uc2 Fired, probationary employee\par \par \uc2 It's a really scary time to navigate as a young public health practitioner, moving away from what I thought was a lifelong career in federal service.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Maino-Vieytes oversaw federal programs aimed at preventing domestic violence, such as funding paid parental leave for local businesses and bringing more vegetation to urban areas. (Recent research suggests improving the environment in which children are raised is an effective way to reduce violence.)\par \par \uc2 Ms. Maino-Vieytes said her office was ''obliterated.'' Only a handful of employees are left.\par \par \uc2 Many local grant recipients need assistance navigating the maze of paperwork and reporting requirements necessary for data collection, and now they won't have the support of those like Ms. Maino-Vieytes. She worries data collection on domestic violence will suffer.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Maino-Vieytes is still job hunting and has applied to more than 120 jobs.\par \par \uc2 Joaquin Baca\par \par \uc2 Albuquerque\par \par \uc2 U.S. Forest Service\par \par \uc2 Took deferred resignation option\par \par \uc2 The American people are now going to lose their water.\par \par \uc2 In the dry Western states around the Colorado River, people fight for water rights. Even the federal government must document its need for water in the West.\par \par \uc2 As a hydrologist, Mr. Baca measured the water consumption of cattle ranchers who hold grazing permits on federal lands to demonstrate their need to have access to the water -- which, by extension, helped the federal government secure its own water rights.\par \par \uc2 But in April, Mr. Baca resigned, leaving behind 17 years of public service and making it harder for the government to document its right to the water on its land.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baca also doesn't know how the government will find water to put out wildfires. Until April, he helped the government purchase water for firefighting from states and private citizens. Now that team is gone, Mr. Baca said.\par \par \uc2 He has since started his own consulting business as a hydrologist.\par \par \uc2 Cynthia Vinson\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 N.I.H.\par \par \uc2 Took early retirement option\par \par \uc2 We're spending all of this money funding research, but if the research doesn't get into practice, what's the point?\par \par \uc2 Ms. Vinson worked on programs designed to help doctors and other public health officials adopt the latest medical research. She worked at N.I.H. for 26 years and now has three jobs because she retired from government earlier than planned.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Ferguson-Ondrey\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\par \par \uc2 Fired, probationary employee\par \par \uc2 There's a cost to all of these cuts. Morale is incredibly low.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ferguson-Ondrey managed employee training for her office, which oversees Head Start, a federal program that helps some of the nation's most underserved children and families. She is now running Wellfed, a community group for federal workers affected by the cuts.\par \par \uc2 David Shapinsky\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\par \par \uc2 Took early retirement option\par \par \uc2 It is not wrong to reorganize. But to simply eliminate positions and expertise without planning is insane.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Shapinsky monitored events that could cause failures in supply chains like those that led to baby formula shortages during the coronavirus pandemic. He worked in the federal government for 15 years and is now retired.\par \par \uc2 Nadia Ford\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\par \par \uc2 Fired, probationary employee\par \par \uc2 I want to be a leader within the government one day.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ford was a Presidential Management Fellow, a program designed to draw young professionals into the federal workforce. But President Trump abruptly ended the program. Ms. Ford is now an internal investigator at a resort company.\par \par \uc2 Patsy Widakuswara\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., area\par \par \uc2 Voice of America\par \par \uc2 Laid off\par \par \uc2 It's like you have somebody in your family who's terminally ill. You keep on trying, but you're also starting to think, 'Yeah, I might lose this person.'\par \par \uc2 Ms. Widakuswara oversaw White House coverage at the federally funded news agency, whose mission is to provide independent reporting to countries with limited press freedom.\par \par \uc2 It was a mission Ms. Widakuswara, a naturalized U.S. citizen who was raised under Indonesia's formerly authoritarian government, was especially dedicated to.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Trump has dismissed the news organization as ''the voice of radical America,'' and his administration has either fired or put on paid leave nearly all of its reporters and staff.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Widakuswara is leading efforts to contest the agency's closure in the courts, but she's also applying for other jobs.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250929el9t00034\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
