\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Walkout Starts After \uc2 Canada Post\uc2  Ends Door-to-Door Service\b0\par\par\uc2 By Vjosa Isai \par \uc2 713 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The government's previous attempts at postal service cuts were politically unpopular, but \uc2 Canada Post\uc2 's dire financial situation has expedited them.\par \par \uc2 Hours after Canada announced on Thursday that it had ordered its beleaguered postal service to end door-to-door mail service, shut down some rural post offices and consolidate its operations, postal workers began a walkout.\par \par \uc2 The government's announcement came as steep financial losses threaten the mail carrier's survival. But it also landed in the midst of protracted labor talks in which the \uc2 Canadian Union of Postal Workers\uc2  has been fighting to preserve jobs and services.\par \par \uc2 Canada Post\uc2 , a government-owned mail service, has lost more than 5 billion Canadian dollars, or $3.6 billion, since 2018, largely because of a drastic drop in letter mail volumes. Hopes that moving parcels could revive the organization have been undermined by the highly competitive nature of that delivery service and new companies' use of nonunionized gig workers.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Canada Post\uc2  is effectively insolvent, and it is facing an existential crisis,'' \uc2 Jo\u235\'00\'EBl Lightbound\uc2 , Canada's minister for public services, said at a news conference on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The government said its cost-cutting measures would help stabilize \uc2 Canada Post\uc2 's finances following multiple bailouts. They are also likely to result in significant layoffs among the service's 68,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 ''Today's announcement will allow us to make the changes needed to restore Canada's postal service for all Canadians by evolving to better meet their needs,'' \uc2 Doug Ettinger\uc2 , \uc2 Canada Post\uc2 's chief executive, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 But the move angered the \uc2 Canadian Union of Postal Workers\uc2 , which said in a statement that its walkout was a direct response ''to the government's attack on our postal service and workers.''\par \par \uc2 It is the second time postal workers have shut down the post office in less than a year. They returned to work last December, but subsequent talks have still not led to a new contract. In May, postal workers started to refuse to work overtime, and this month they stopped delivering advertising leaflets.\par \par \uc2 ''What leverage the union had at the bargaining table has now been significantly undermined,'' said Stephanie Ross, a professor of labor studies at \uc2 McMaster University\uc2  in Hamilton, Ontario. ''These two parties are in an existential battle for survival.''\par \par \uc2 At-home postal delivery had already been a fading tradition across much of Canada, with only about 25 percent of Canadians getting mail at their homes. For most Canadians, mail is placed in individual boxes that are part of community mailboxes near their homes or apartments.\par \par \uc2 Still, the elimination of remaining door-to-door service translates to about four million addresses, the government said.\par \par \uc2 ''The data was screaming at us that \uc2 Canada Post\uc2  was desperate to be reorganized and restructured,'' said Ian Lee, a business professor at \uc2 Carleton University\uc2  in Ottawa who has studied the postal service.\par \par \uc2 Canada Post\uc2  had been moving toward ending door-to-door delivery until \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2 , the former prime minister, made it a prominent issue in his successful 2015 election campaign. One of his first acts after taking office was to order the post office to stop its shift to community mailboxes.\par \par \uc2 Since then, the volume of letter mail has plunged, particularly during and after the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 While Canadians are not in favor of fully ending door-to-door delivery, according to a June poll by Angus Reid Institute, a nonprofit polling firm, they are more supportive of changes to \uc2 Canada Post\uc2 's operations and reductions in its service than they were in previous years, given the postal service's precarious future.\par \par \uc2 ''The political decision is that we can't, Canadians can't, be footing an ever-growing bill year after year,'' Mr. Lightbound said.\par \par \uc2 No firm date has been set for the end of at-home mail delivery, but the changes will be gradually implemented, officials said.\par \par \uc2 Other measures include transporting certain mail by ground rather than by air to save on costs. The government also revoked a ban on closing rural post offices that was introduced in 1994. At that time, those closings were also politically contentious.\par \par \uc2 Ian Austen contributed reporting from Ottawa.\par \par \uc2 Ian Austen contributed reporting from Ottawa.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250927el9r0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
