\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Fee for Visa Tilts Scales To Big Tech\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ryan Mac and Natallie Rocha \par \uc2 886 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Silicon Valley start-ups said they were concerned they would be disproportionately hurt by the new visa fee for skilled foreign workers, given their limited resources.\par \par \uc2 After President Trump signed a proclamation on Friday to add a $100,000 fee for new applicants of H-1B visas for skilled foreign workers, two technology leaders reacted in opposite ways.\par \par \uc2 Selin Kocalar, 21, the chief operating officer of Delve, an artificial intelligence start-up in San Francisco, learned of the change from a new employee who had just been approved for the H-1B visa. She said she was grappling with what the fee meant for hiring at her 23-person company, which has raised $35 million in funding.\par \par \uc2 ''As a start-up, you're always tight for cash,'' she said. ''So you can't go out and spend a bunch of money or have that kind of luxury that you'd see at a bigger company.''\par \par \uc2 In contrast, \uc2 Reed Hastings\uc2 , the chairman and co-founder of \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , did not blink. The $100,000 fee ''is a great solution,'' he posted on social media on Sunday. ''It will mean H-1B is used just for very high value jobs.'' \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , which has approximately 14,000 employees, pulled in more than $11 billion in revenue and $3.1 billion in profit in the most recent quarter.\par \par \uc2 The different responses show how the Trump administration's visa change is rippling through the tech industry -- which uses H-1Bs to hire thousands of software engineers, artificial intelligence specialists and others -- in a way that may create haves and have-nots. While the biggest tech companies have the money to absorb the new fee, start-ups are concerned about their ability to attract and pay for talent, particularly when they have limited funding and agonize over every dollar spent.\par \par \uc2 The change may hit at the heart of the U.S. tech ecosystem, tilting the scales in favor of incumbent giants with billions of dollars. Silicon Valley relies on a steady stream of start-ups to advance new ideas and technologies, with some of the ventures eventually growing into behemoths.\par \par \uc2 ''This will be a disproportionate hit on smaller companies because we cannot compete with \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and \uc2 Meta\uc2 ,'' said Aizada Marat, the chief executive of Alma, an immigration legal services start-up in Palo Alto, Calif., which helps companies handle employee visas.\par \par \uc2 If the $100,000 fee imposed by the government sticks, Mrs. Marat said her company, which has raised $5.5 million in funding and relies on foreign workers, will not be able to hire people through the H-1B visa program.\par \par \uc2 The change could ultimately hurt U.S. leadership in tech, some entrepreneurs said, especially as the country engages in a heated contest over artificial intelligence with China.\par \par \uc2 Jihan Merlin, the head of immigration strategy at Alma, said the new visa fee was unlikely to affect the country overnight, ''but policy changes like this make people think twice about bringing their companies to the U.S.,'' she said. ''And over time that's going to change our competitiveness as a country.''\par \par \uc2 A typical H-1B visa costs companies about $10,000 including legal fees and administrative costs, Ms. Merlin said.\par \par \uc2 Tech leaders and politicians have long agreed that the United States needed other ways to attract and retain talent and that the current system for doling out authorization for skilled foreign workers was ripe for reform. The H-1B program has relied on a lottery system and capped visas to 85,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 In June 2024, Mr. Trump acknowledged the issues during his presidential campaign and proposed that anyone who graduated from a U.S. university or junior college should receive a green card to live and work in the country. Some of his closest backers recoiled at the idea.\par \par \uc2 ''You need a pool of people to work for your companies,'' he said on ''The ''All-In Podcast,'' which is hosted by several tech investors. ''You have great companies, and they have to be smart people.''\par \par \uc2 Trevor Traina, a San Francisco-based founder who served as an ambassador during Mr. Trump's first presidency, called the new H-1B changes ''an opening bid'' to change the conversation on immigration.\par \par \uc2 ''The tech industry would benefit from more visas for skilled foreign workers and I'm sure many are hoping this will lead to a good long-term solution,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Others are not so sure. The policy change disrupts the pipeline of top tech talent coming into the United States and compromises its edge in the global A.I. race, said \uc2 Bilal Zuberi\uc2 , 49, a managing partner at Red Glass Ventures.\par \par \uc2 ''Disrupting that is really shooting ourselves in the foot,'' Mr. Zuberi said. He estimated that about 30 to 40 companies that he has backed will be affected by these changes.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zuberi said start-ups may find workarounds to the new H-1B visa fee, including by using the extraordinary talent, or O-1 visa, program or by setting up offices in other countries, like Canada, to hire remote employees.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think the answer for these companies is going to be 'Oh, why don't we just hire Americans?''' he said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250924el9o0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
