\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Spirit\uc2  to Furlough a Third Of Its Flight Attendants\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 578 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The budget airline said it would furlough hundreds of employee as it waded through its second bankruptcy in a year.\par \par \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2  plans to furlough a third of its flight attendants as it seeks to cut costs in a bid to survive.\par \par \uc2 The airline, which in August filed for its second bankruptcy in a year, said on Monday that it plans to put about 1,800 of about 5,200 flight attendants on extended leave by December. The airline had said previously that it would use the bankruptcy to retrench, cutting its fleet of planes and limiting flying to its most important markets.\par \par \uc2 ''We recognize the impact of this decision on affected team members, and we are committed to treating them with care and respect during this process,'' the airline said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Spirit\uc2  rose in recent decades to become one of the most recognizable, if polarizing, budget airlines in the United States. But it has also become a victim of its own success, with major airlines now offering competing bare-bones fares and the U.S. market flooded with flights to the leisure destinations \uc2 Spirit\uc2  traditionally served.\par \par \uc2 In a memo to flight attendants, \uc2 John Bendoraitis\uc2 , the chief operating officer at \uc2 Spirit\uc2 , said that the airline had so far been able to avoid involuntary furloughs, thanks to flight attendants who had opted to take voluntary leave. At times, as many as 1,000 attendants had signed up, with more than 800 currently on leave, he said. Those 800 employees would be among those placed on extended leave in December.\par \par \uc2 The airline will seek more volunteers this week to go on furlough starting Nov. 1, according to a copy of the memo seen by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . But \uc2 Spirit\uc2  will then put more employees on involuntary furlough a month later.\par \par \uc2 ''This is hard news, and we understand it affects not only you and your peers, but also your families,'' Mr. Bendoraitis said. ''It is not lost on our leadership team, and we are deeply grateful for each and every one of you for the hard work you've put in to support our airline.''\par \par \uc2 The union representing Spirit flight attendants, the \uc2 Association of Flight Attendants-CWA\uc2 , said in a message to its members that voluntary furloughs would last for either six months or one year, and flight attendants will be able to retain their health care benefits at the same rate. When \uc2 Spirit\uc2  moves to involuntary leaves, the airline will start with those attendants with the least experience. Those placed on involuntary leave will retain medical benefits at the same rates for 90 days, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 ''We have been able to mitigate harm for our work group until this point,'' the union said in its memo. ''The problem is that the significant reduction of aircraft and flight hours requires a much higher reduction in force and the company is clear that a furlough is necessary.''\par \par \uc2 Spirit\uc2  first filed for bankruptcy in November after losing more than $2 billion in five years, challenged by myriad factors, including rising competition among airlines within the United States and problems with a jet engine used widely in its fleet. The company was focused on reducing debt and raising capital and exited bankruptcy in March. But it filed for bankruptcy again last month with the aim of making more strategic changes, including cutting more costs.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250923el9n0003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
