\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  Strike on Hold After Unions' Request to Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stefanos Chen \par \uc2 919 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A strike by workers on the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2 , the United States' largest commuter rail service, has been averted, and could be postponed for several months.\par \par \uc2 A strike on the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2 , the biggest commuter rail service in the United States, will be avoided for at least several months, after the unions threatening a work stoppage took the unusual step of seeking the White House's intervention.\par \par \uc2 Unions representing thousands of workers for the railroad, which carries more than 270,000 passengers a day between Long Island and New York City, could have walked off the job as early as Thursday. A federal mediation agency that had been overseeing negotiations with the \uc2 Metropolitan Transportation Authority\uc2 , the state agency that runs the railroad, last month permitted the workers to proceed with a strike.\par \par \uc2 But on Monday, representatives of the five unions -- including engineers, machinists and signalmen -- said they had asked President Trump to form a panel known as a presidential emergency board, which would postpone the strike and seek a contract settlement.\par \par \uc2 ''This action does not mean a strike won't happen, but it does mean a strike won't happen now,'' said Gilman Lang, the general chairman of the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2 , one of the unions.\par \par \uc2 The soonest a strike could now occur is mid-January, but further federal intervention could delay it until May, according to union officials.\par \par \uc2 The walkout could have caused major disruptions for riders traveling to and from major airports, schools and business hubs in Manhattan, Brooklyn and Queens.\par \par \uc2 The M.T.A. announced a contingency plan involving limited shuttle buses, but said last week that it did not have the capacity to fully replace the sprawling service. Some commuters might have been compelled to drive into the busiest parts of Manhattan, which would mean paying the city's new congestion pricing fee -- $9 a day for most vehicles. Many drivers from the New York City suburbs, including Long Island, have opposed the toll.\par \par \uc2 The unions, representing over 3,000 railroad workers, had threatened to strike unless they received pay raises that exceeded those negotiated by other M.T.A. workers.\par \par \uc2 Many other divisions of the M.T.A., including unions that represent other \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  employees, have already agreed to a 9.5 percent wage increase over the next three years.\par \par \uc2 The holdout unions said they are seeking an additional 6.5 percent increase in a fourth year, which they said would help keep up with inflation. The unions have not received a raise since 2022, and contract negotiations have been in mediation for more than a year, the unions said.\par \par \uc2 The M.T.A. has argued that the unions have not made serious efforts to negotiate in recent months, including about work rules that the authority said inflated salaries and created inefficiencies in work schedules.\par \par \uc2 For instance, a train engineer who operates a diesel train but switches to an electric train during the same shift can receive an extra full day's pay under current rules. The unions have said that they are focused on salary discussions, not on changes to work rules.\par \par \uc2 The railroad's work force is already among the highest paid in the nation, with engineers earning on average more than $160,000 per year, according to M.T.A. officials.\par \par \uc2 ''After months of radio silence, these outlier unions have finally admitted that they weren't serious about negotiating,'' John J. McCarthy, the authority's chief of policy and external relations, said in a statement. ''They never had a plan to resolve this at the bargaining table.''\par \par \uc2 The strike would have been the first to take place on the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  since 1994, when a two-day suspension sent riders scrambling for alternatives.\par \par \uc2 The unions' decision to request federal intervention is rare.\par \par \uc2 According to a 1920s federal labor law that regulates railroads, the unions, the governor or the president could call for the intervention of an emergency board, which would review the dispute.\par \par \uc2 But that request is almost never made by the workers, according to Jim Louis, a vice president with the engineers' union. The unions requested the emergency board partly because it appeared that Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , who controls the M.T.A., was unlikely to seek the president's aid.\par \par \uc2 ''The five unions decided to be the grown-up in the room,'' Mr. Louis said.\par \par \uc2 At a news conference last week, the governor blamed the White House for allowing a strike to proceed. In August, the \uc2 National Mediation Board\uc2 , a federal agency, released the unions from ongoing mediation, effectively enabling a strike as early as this week.\par \par \uc2 The White House and the board that ended the mediation did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The strike postponement comes just a week before Long Island hosts the Ryder Cup, a major international golfing event that is expected to rely heavily on rail service.\par \par \uc2 But the delay sets the stage for a potentially messier contract dispute next spring, during an election year for Governor Hochul.\par \par \uc2 At a news conference on Monday afternoon, she called the holdout unions' demands ''greedy'' and a threat to the economy.\par \par \uc2 ''We have to get away from the strike language,'' she said, ''and the White House and others should be using their power to say: 'You're not allowed to strike. You cannot strike; work it out at the table.'''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250916el9g0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
