\par\par \uc2 Money and Business/Financial Desk; SECTBU\par \b \uc2 911,000\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kristen Bayrakdarian \par \uc2 441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The drop in the revised number of jobs created in the U.S. economy in the 12 months through March.\par \par \uc2 Data released on Tuesday shows that employers added 911,000 fewer jobs last year and early this year than previously reported. That means the economy added only about half as many jobs -- roughly 850,000 -- in the final months of the Biden administration and early months of President Trump's second term as the monthly reports had indicated.\par \par \uc2 The numbers were revised just weeks after Mr. Trump fired the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 ' top official when a monthly report showed unexpectedly weak hiring in July and large downward revisions to job growth.\par \par \uc2 The new data, reported by my colleague Ben Casselman, continues a drumbeat of gloomy news about employment. The statistics bureau reported that employers added just 22,000 jobs in August, well below forecasts. At the same time, the unemployment rate increased to 4.3 percent, its highest level in nearly four years.\par \par \uc2 The yearly revision has led many economists to conclude that more recent jobs reports showing gains may also have been overstated -- a concern for Federal Reserve policymakers, who rely on data about the labor market to determine what to do about interest rates. The Fed is now widely expected this week to resume cutting interest rates, which have been set in a range of 4.25 to 4.5 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The Fed, which has a dual mandate of promoting employment and keeping prices stable, has been wary of reducing borrowing costs too quickly. Fed officials were concerned that Mr. Trump's tariffs would intensify inflation risks. At the same time, the job market seemed to be on a sound footing.\par \par \uc2 But the revised job count, along with the fact that prices have not gone up as much as anticipated when Mr. Trump announced his trade policy this year, has painted a new picture of the health of the economy.\par \par \uc2 ''These are like Manolo Blahnik shoes.''\par \par \uc2 Richard Kramer, a financial analyst with Arete Research, on the new thinner and smaller iPhone Air.\par \par \uc2 ''For the first time, I was like: 'Oh, this is ridiculous. I'm not spending more than $7 on a coffee.'''\par \par \uc2 Dyanna Hough, a hairstylist from Holly Springs, N.C., on the higher prices and other changes at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''People feel a certain sense of affinity and a sense of relief when they see these stores.''\par \par \uc2 Gavin Whitelaw, the executive director of the Edwin O. Reischauer Institute of Japanese Studies at Harvard, on why convenience stores in Japan are so special.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250914el9e0006w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
