\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Unusual Climate Case Against Oil Giants Is Dismissed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Zraick \par \uc2 889 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Citing laws more commonly used against organized crime, the lawsuit argued that fossil fuel companies were responsible for devastating hurricane damage in Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 An unusual lawsuit seeking to hold fossil fuel companies responsible for the hurricane devastation in Puerto Rico in 2017 has been dismissed by a judge. The case had accused the companies of racketeering, a claim more commonly used against organized crime.\par \par \uc2 But the ruling left open the door for a partial appeal, which the plaintiffs -- dozens of cities and towns scattered across the archipelago -- said they intended to file.\par \par \uc2 ''We will appeal this decision, and as these climate cases move to the higher courts, we are confident they will deliver justice for Puerto Rico,'' said Marc Grossman, an attorney at the firm Milberg who represents the municipalities.\par \par \uc2 Judge Silvia Carre\u241\'00\'F1o-Coll of the U.S. District Court for the District of Puerto Rico ruled that the lawsuit was prohibited by a four-year statute of limitations, since the storms occurred in 2017 but the case wasn't filed until 2022. ''Plaintiffs were not diligent'' in filing on time, the judge wrote in the 125-page ruling. The plaintiffs said they disputed that interpretation of the statute of limitations, citing a recommendation by a different judge.\par \par \uc2 Judge Carre\u241\'00\'F1o-Coll, who was nominated by President Trump in 2019, also ruled that the plaintiffs had failed to establish that all of the defendants had sufficient connections to Puerto Rico to justify the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The judge wrote in her finding that ''the court is not insensitive to the plight of the people of Puerto Rico'' from the devastating twin hurricanes in 2017. She also noted that she wasn't ruling on the merits of the novel racketeering legal theories at the heart of the case.\par \par \uc2 That distinction is important, said \uc2 Richard Wiles\uc2 , the president of the Center for Climate Integrity, which supports the litigation. It means ''the door is still open for others to bring similar racketeering claims against the fossil fuel industry,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Hurricanes Irma and Maria hit Puerto Rico with brutal force in quick succession in September 2017, almost entirely wiping out the fragile power grid. The Puerto Rican government estimates that 2,975 people died because of the storms or their long-term effects, a figure that has been hotly debated.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit, Bayam\u243\'00\'F3n et al v. \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  et al, had grown to include 37 municipalities suing nine companies and the \uc2 American Petroleum Institute\uc2 , a trade association. Judge Carre\u241\'00\'F1o-Coll dismissed many of the claims ''with prejudice,'' a legal term of art that means the cases cannot be refiled. Those included all the claims against \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2 , \uc2 Shell\uc2 , \uc2 Chevron\uc2 , \uc2 BP\uc2 , \uc2 ConocoPhillips\uc2  and Motiva.\par \par \uc2 Theodore J. Boutrous Jr., a lawyer representing \uc2 Chevron\uc2 , applauded the decision. ''The civil justice system exists to resolve current disputes, not to attack and second-guess policy resolutions that have been widely discussed and grappled with openly since Lyndon B. Johnson was president,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 But the claims against \uc2 Occidental\uc2 , \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  and \uc2 BHP\uc2  were dismissed without prejudice, so some claims against them could be brought again.\par \par \uc2 The outcome for the \uc2 American Petroleum Institute\uc2  was also mixed. The racketeering claim was dismissed with prejudice, but the judge left open the possibility for claims of federal antitrust violations, as well as for claims under the laws of Puerto Rico, rather than under federal law. Puerto Rico is a U.S. territory and has its own court system, as states do.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Meyers, the institute's senior vice president and general counsel, said the case showed that climate policy should be decided by Congress, not the courts. ''This ongoing, coordinated campaign to wage meritless, politicized lawsuits against a foundational American industry and its workers is nothing more than a distraction from important national conversations and an enormous waste of taxpayer resources,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit had argued that the companies were responsible for a large share of global greenhouse gas emissions and colluded to deceive the public about the consequences. The emissions of greenhouse gases like carbon dioxide from the burning of fossil fuels is a primary cause of climate change.\par \par \uc2 Since 2017, state and local governments around the country have brought some three dozen lawsuits in state courts making the argument that fossil fuel companies were deceptive and should bear some costs of climate change. So far, those cases have faced mixed outcomes and none have gone to trial. On Thursday, the Trump administration urged the Supreme Court to examine the lawsuits in a brief regarding a case brought by Boulder, Colo.\par \par \uc2 The decision in Puerto Rico to include racketeering charges was groundbreaking, as was the focus on specific weather events -- the 2017 hurricanes. Only one other lawsuit, by Hoboken, N.J., has included a racketeering claim, though it was based on a state law, not federal.\par \par \uc2 Since the Puerto Rico lawsuit was filed, two other suits have focused on a discrete event: the 2021 ''heat dome'' in the Pacific Northwest. A lawsuit by Multnomah County, Ore., seeks to hold energy companies responsible for the heat wave, which killed at least 69 county residents. A similar suit was filed in May by the daughter of a woman who died in her car in Seattle during the heat wave.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250914el9e0004u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
