\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi and Reuters \par \uc2 521 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LABOR\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Reaches Deal\par \par \uc2 With Aerospace Union\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and a union that represents about 3,200 employees who build military aircraft and equipment in the St. Louis area reached a deal on Wednesday on a proposed contract that could end a five-week strike.\par \par \uc2 The workers walked off the job on Aug. 4 after rejecting an earlier deal between the company and the union, the International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers District 837.\par \par \uc2 The union said its new five-year contract proposal included improved raises and the restoration of a signing bonus. Officials did not publicly share many details, but said members would soon receive more information.\par \par \uc2 Workers will vote on the contract on Friday morning. The deal that they rejected included improvements to medical benefits, pensions, overtime and work-life balance. It also would have raised wages 40 percent, on average, over four years.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said the new deal would raise wages 45 percent, on average.\par \par \uc2 ''It remains the best deal we've ever offered to I.A.M. 837, and we encourage our team to vote yes so we can get back to work building amazing products for our customers,'' Dan Gillian, a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  vice president, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Tensions between the workers and the company have been escalating. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said last week that it was preparing to hire permanent replacements for some of the strikers.\par \par \uc2 The St. Louis plants are open, and workers who are not on strike continue to build, test and deliver some products, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said. The company's defense unit brought in about $23 billion in revenue last year, more than a third of the company's total sales. NIRAJ CHOKSHI\par \par \uc2 EARNINGS\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2  Raises Forecast\par \par \uc2 For Annual Core Sales\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2  raised its annual core sales forecast on Thursday for the second straight time, banking on resilient demand for fresh produce and other essentials from cautious Americans cutting back on eating out.\par \par \uc2 The company has focused on promotions and maintaining low prices to meet rising customer demand for value. On Thursday, \uc2 Kroger\uc2  said it had lowered prices on more than 3,500 products this year, up from the more than 2,000 it disclosed in June.\par \par \uc2 ''Low- and middle-income households are really looking for deals ... And they're buying more private label products. They're also eating out less,'' the interim chief executive, \uc2 Ron Sargent\uc2 , said.\par \par \uc2 Its peers, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and \uc2 Dollar General\uc2 , have raised their annual sales forecasts, indicating wealthier shoppers were also flocking to their stores worried about prices.\par \par \uc2 ''Food-at-home sits favorably in times of consumer stress and in terms of tariff exposure, and \uc2 Kroger\uc2  has opened FY'25 with consecutive guidance increases,'' said Bill Kirk, analyst with \uc2 Roth Capital Partners\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , which is relatively less exposed to U.S. tariffs, now expects full-year comparable sales to increase in the range of 2.7 percent to 3.4 percent, versus its prior forecast of a 2.25 percent to 3.25 percent rise.\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2  also said it expects to increase store openings by 30 percent in 2026. Reuters\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250912el9c00077\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
