\par\par \uc2 STOCKS &AMP; BONDS\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Disappointing Jobs Report Pushes Wall Street to a High\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 690 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks rose to more records on Tuesday after the latest update on the job market bolstered Wall Street's hopes for a slowdown that's deep enough to get the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut interest rates, but not so overwhelming that it causes a recession.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 rose 0.3 percent and squeaked past its high set last week. The Dow Jones industrial average climbed 196 points, or 0.4 percent, while the Nasdaq composite gained 0.4 percent. They likewise set records.\par \par \uc2 Traders have become convinced that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will cut its main interest rate for the first time this year at its meeting next week to prop up the slowing job market. A report on Tuesday offered the latest signal of weakness, when the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  said its prior count of jobs across the country through March may have been too high by 911,000, or 0.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 That was before President Trump shocked the economy and financial markets in April by rolling out tariffs on countries worldwide.\par \par \uc2 The bet on Wall Street is that such data will convince Fed officials that the job market is the bigger problem now for the economy than the threat of inflation worsening because of Mr. Trump's tariffs. That would push them to cut interest rates, a move that would stimulate the economy but could also send inflation higher.\par \par \uc2 A lot is riding on Wall Street's hope that the job market is slowing by just the right amount: Investors have already sent U.S. stock prices to records because of it. Inflation also needs to stay at a reasonable level, even though it looks tough to get below the Fed's target of 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Traders are unanimously expecting a rate cut next week, but they pared their forecasts for a deeper-than-usual reduction after Tuesday's revision for U.S. job growth. That caused a slight recovery for Treasury yields after their sharp recent slide.\par \par \uc2 ''The more likely course is for the Fed to deliver an October and December cut rather than trying to deliver a catch-up cut in September,'' said \uc2 Brian Jacobsen\uc2 , chief economist at Annex Wealth Management.\par \par \uc2 Coming reports on inflation due on Wednesday and Thursday could alter expectations further. Hotter-than-expected readings could put the Fed in a worst-case scenario and make cuts to rates less palatable.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, \uc2 UnitedHealth Group\uc2  climbed 8.6 percent after saying it plans to tell investors and analysts that it's sticking with its profit forecast for 2025. That helped it trim its loss for the year so far, which came into the day at 36.7 percent, as insurers across the industry have contended with soaring medical costs.\par \par \uc2 Nebius Group\uc2 , a Dutch company working in artificial-intelligence infrastructure, saw its stock that trades in the United States soar 49.4 percent after announcing a contract to deliver G.P.U. services to \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 . The contract could be worth between $17.4 billion and $19.4 billion, and it runs through 2031.\par \par \uc2 Fox\uc2  dropped 6.1 percent after \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2 's family said they've reached a deal on control of the 94-year-old mogul's media empire after his death. The agreement ensures that there will be no change in direction at Fox News, the most popular network for Mr. Trump and American conservatives.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  slipped 1.5 percent after unveiling its next generation of iPhones.\par \par \uc2 In stock markets abroad, France's CAC 40 rose 0.2 percent as the market remained relatively calm even though its government is facing a crisis of confidence after legislators voted to oust another prime minister. It and other governments around the world, including the United States, are facing increased scrutiny on how they plan to pay for their spending.\par \par \uc2 Indexes were mixed across the rest of Europe and in Asia.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, the yield on the 10-year Treasury rose to 4.08 percent from 4.05 percent late Monday.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250910el9a0004y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
