\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Travel Chaos Hits London as Days-Long Underground Strike Takes Effect\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Castle \par \uc2 908 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Underground stations were closed and buses were crammed with commuters grappling with a walkout by transit workers that is expected to continue until Friday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 London Underground\uc2  shuddered largely to a halt on Monday, as one of the world's biggest public transit systems was hit by a labor strike that is scheduled to continue until Friday morning, affecting millions of travelers.\par \par \uc2 Stations on the underground network, known as the Tube, were shuttered, and buses and trains running above ground were more crowded than usual, while many commuters walked or cycled into the city. Trams and the Elizabeth line, a relatively new route running from the east to the west of London, were operating on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Still, with more than four million Tube journeys made every weekday, stores, bars, restaurants and other businesses were braced for financial losses if, as expected, more people work remotely and avoid central London.\par \par \uc2 Against a backdrop of rising food and energy costs, labor unrest has afflicted several parts of Britain in recent years, including a long-running dispute by garbage collectors in Birmingham, the country's second most populous city.\par \par \uc2 The strike on the \uc2 London Underground\uc2  mainly centers on a push to improve conditions and eventually to move to a 32-hour, four-day workweek.\par \par \uc2 Drivers of Underground trains earned around 65,000 pounds in 2024, worth about $87,000 today, a salary that is above the average in Britain. But many work undesirable hours, with some beginning at 4 a.m. or finishing at 1 a.m., and spend their shifts underground without any access to natural light.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Union of Rail, Maritime and Transport Workers\uc2 , which represents a range of transportation workers, said it was pushing for \uc2 Transport for London\uc2 , the city's transit authority, to start reducing the working week for drivers to 32 hours from 35.\par \par \uc2 The union argues that the health and life expectancy of its members was affected by the irregular shift patterns, exposure to dust particles and the psychological impact of suicides on the underground system.\par \par \uc2 The union also argues that the transit authority ran a surplus of about 160 million pounds ($216 million) last year and that the number of employees working on the network is 2,000 less than in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Transport for London\uc2  has rejected the union's demands.\par \par \uc2 ''Any reduction in working hours is impractical and absolutely unaffordable, with costs that could run into the hundreds of millions of pounds,'' the transit authority said in a statement, adding that it had made a ''fair offer'' of a 3.4 percent pay increase for all \uc2 London Underground\uc2  staff members, in line with inflation in February.\par \par \uc2 Claire Mann, the chief operating officer, called on the union to suspend the strike and to put its offer to members.\par \par \uc2 With companies often adapting work practices since the Covid-19 pandemic, and many more people able to work from home, London's travel disruption may have less economic impact than it would have in the past.\par \par \uc2 But, blaming ''insufficient transport options,'' Coldplay rescheduled two concerts at Wembley Stadium -- one example of the strike's impact on the entertainment and hospitality sectors, which are expected to take a financial hit.\par \par \uc2 ''At a time when the nighttime economy is still extremely fragile, this level of disruption could not be more untimely,'' Michael Kill, chief executive officer of the \uc2 Night Time Industries Association\uc2 , a group that promotes entertainment and nightlife businesses, said in a statement before the strike.\par \par \uc2 ''Our sector is already under immense pressure from rising costs and reduced consumer confidence,'' Mr. Kill said. ''To now face a week of transport chaos will devastate businesses, staff and customers who depend on safe and reliable late-night travel.''\par \par \uc2 On Monday Downing Street bemoaned the effect of the disruption on Londoners but took a neutral stance, urging both sides to come to the negotiating table.\par \par \uc2 That contrasts with the position taken earlier this year by the former deputy prime minister, Angela Rayner, who angered a leading trade union when she urged striking sanitation workers in Birmingham to accept a deal and end their strike.\par \par \uc2 Any economic dislocation will be unwelcome to the government, which is struggling to re-energize a sluggish economy.\par \par \uc2 After taking office last year, Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  offered above-inflation pay increases to some public sector workers, and offered to raise the average salary of doctors more than 20 percent over two years, ending a long-running series of strikes. However, that dispute has rumbled on and doctors walked out again in July, while several other groups of workers have threatened or taken strike action.\par \par \uc2 Kemi Badenoch\uc2 , leader of the opposition Conservative Party, said in a statement that Mr. Starmer had given ''huge inflation-busting pay rises to the unions, promising this would end strikes,'' adding, ''but here we are a year later with doctors voting to strike for the second time this summer and London Tube drivers have walked out, piling more misery on all of us.''\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Sadiq Khan\uc2 , the mayor of London who also chairs \uc2 Transport for London\uc2 , said that ''nobody wants to see strike action or disruption for Londoners,'' adding that ''strikes have a serious impact on London's businesses and commuters.'' He urged the trade union and employer to ''get around the table to resolve this matter,'' and reopen the network.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250909el9900046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
