\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Meta Suit Alleges Flaw In Security\b0\par\par\uc2 By Cecilia Kang \par \uc2 1,261 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The former head of security for \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  filed a lawsuit on Monday accusing \uc2 Meta\uc2  of ignoring major security and privacy flaws that put billions of the messaging app's users at risk, the latest in a string of whistle-blower allegations against the social media giant.\par \par \uc2 In the lawsuit filed in the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of California, Attaullah Baig claimed that thousands of \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  and \uc2 Meta\uc2  employees could gain access to sensitive user data including profile pictures, location, group memberships and contact lists. \uc2 Meta\uc2 , which owns \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2 , also failed to adequately address the hacking of more than 100,000 accounts each day and rejected his proposals for security fixes, according to the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baig tried to warn \uc2 Meta\uc2 's top leaders, including its chief executive, \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , that users were being harmed by the security weaknesses, according to the lawsuit. In response, his managers retaliated and fired him in February, he claims.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baig, who is represented by the whistle-blower organization Psst.org and the law firm Schonbrun, Seplow, Harris, Hoffman & Zeldes, argued in the suit that the actions violated a privacy settlement \uc2 Meta\uc2  reached with the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  in 2019, as well as securities laws that require companies to disclose risks to shareholders.\par \par \uc2 ''There are just so many harms that the users face,'' Mr. Baig said in an interview last week, adding that he had also alerted the F.T.C. and the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  to his concerns. ''This is about holding \uc2 Meta\uc2  accountable and putting the interests of users first.''\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  pushed back on his claims. ''Sadly, this is a familiar playbook in which a former employee is dismissed for poor performance and then goes public with distorted claims that misrepresent the ongoing hard work of our team,'' said \uc2 Carl Woog\uc2 , a spokesman for \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2 . ''Security is an adversarial space, and we pride ourselves in building on our strong record of protecting people's privacy.''\par \par \uc2 In 2019, the company, then known as \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , agreed to pay a $5 billion fine and strengthen its privacy policies to settle charges that it had mishandled users' information by allowing a British political consulting firm, Cambridge Analytica, to harvest data without permission.\par \par \uc2 ''Privacy is more central than ever to our vision for the future,'' Mr. Zuckerberg said in a companywide meeting after the settlement was announced with the F.T.C. ''And we're going to change the way that we operate across the whole company, from the leadership down and the ground up. We're going to change how we build products, and if we don't, then we're going to be held accountable for it.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baig is the latest whistle-blower to come forward accusing \uc2 Meta\uc2  -- which also owns \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  -- of wrongdoing related to privacy, child safety and the spread of disinformation on its main platforms.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, another whistle-blower organization, Whistleblower Aid, said six former and current \uc2 Meta\uc2  employees had disclosed to Congress and federal regulators that the company put children at harm on its virtual reality products. Two of the former employees plan to testify on Tuesday in a Senate hearing on child safety. They plan to say \uc2 Meta\uc2  deleted or doctored internal safety research of children as young as 10 who were exposed to sexual abuse grooming, sexual harassment and violence on the company's virtual reality platform, according to Whistleblower Aid.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  said those claims were ''nonsense'' and based on ''selectively leaked internal documents that were picked specifically to craft a false narrative.''\par \par \uc2 In March, Sarah Wynn Williams, a former leader of global policy, published a book, ''Careless People,'' that describes a series of incendiary allegations of sexual harassment and other inappropriate behavior by senior executives. (\uc2 Meta\uc2  won a legal victory blocking the promotion of the book and has denied the allegations it details.)\par \par \uc2 And in late 2021, Frances Haugen, another former employee, testified before Congress that the company had knowingly created products that harmed teenagers, among other safety concerns, presenting thousands of pages of supporting internal documents to support her claims.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 's vice president for public policy at the time, \uc2 Nick Clegg\uc2 , said her accusations were ''misleading.'' He said that the platform reflected ''the good, the bad and ugly of humanity'' and that it was trying to ''mitigate the bad, reduce it and amplify the good.''\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  bought \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  in 2014 for $19 billion. Many of its three billion users turn to the app for its perceived security benefits, including encryption, which scrambles messages so they can be deciphered only by the sender and the intended recipient.\par \par \uc2 In June, \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  unveiled ads in some parts of the app, a move that included optional data sharing that some users said was at odds with its longstanding stated philosophy toward privacy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baig joined \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  in January 2021 as head of security. Soon after, he conducted a ''red-teaming'' exercise, where employees posed as attackers trying to exploit the service, according to the lawsuit. Roughly 1,500 \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  employees had unrestricted access to sensitive user data, which was a violation of the company's 2020 privacy settlement with the F.T.C., according to the suit.\par \par \uc2 As part of the settlement with the F.T.C., \uc2 Meta\uc2  had agreed to stronger privacy practices that included regular independent auditing of its systems, limiting sharing of data and putting in place a clear and comprehensive data security program for its apps.\par \par \uc2 For over a year, Mr. Baig repeatedly tried to raise the issue to his supervisor, according to the suit, but was told to ''focus on less critical application security tasks.''\par \par \uc2 In October 2022, Mr. Baig documented a list of ''critical cybersecurity problems'' that he considered to be violating the F.T.C. order and securities laws, according to the suit. \uc2 Meta\uc2  was failing to address account hacking and wasn't keeping track of all the data it was collecting on \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  users, the suit claims.\par \par \uc2 ''We have a fiduciary responsibility to protect our users and their data,'' Mr. Baig wrote in the document he presented to top \uc2 WhatsApp\uc2  executives, according to the suit. ''The penalties can be severe both in terms of brand damages and fines.''\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  blocked several security efforts by Mr. Baig's team, according to the lawsuit, including a proposed feature that required additional login approval for account recovery and one that prevented profile pictures from being downloaded from the service.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Mr. Baig said that every day his team saw ''real-world, actual harm happening,'' such as ''account compromises, scraping impersonation, journalists being targeted.''\par \par \uc2 In December, Mr. Baig informed Mr. Zuckerberg that he had filed a complaint with the S.E.C. stating that the company had failed to inform investors of cybersecurity risks, according to the suit.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baig's managers retaliated with threats of firing and withholding compensation, the suit claims. His performance reviews became more negative, and in February, he was fired.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baig filed a complaint with the \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2  in April against \uc2 Meta\uc2  for retaliation against his reporting of security problems.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baig said in the interview that working at \uc2 Meta\uc2  had been his ''dream job'' because of the company's scale and the ability to solve problems that affected billions of users.\par \par \uc2 But now he thinks that ''\uc2 Meta\uc2  treats its users like they are just numbers on some dashboard,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Eli Tan contributed reporting from San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Eli Tan contributed reporting from San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250909el990003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
