\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Air Canada Flight Attendants Turn Down Proposed Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Austen \par \uc2 777 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 But the vote will not lead to a repeat of the walkout that snarled air travel in Canada last month.\par \par \uc2 About 10,000 flight attendants at \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  resoundingly rejected a proposed contract that was negotiated last month after they defied federal back-to-work orders.\par \par \uc2 The union and airline said, however, that there would not be a resumption of the walkout that shut down Canada's main air carrier for five days last month.\par \par \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  said in a statement that it and the union had anticipated that the contract might be rejected and had reached an agreement not to restart the strike and lockout if that happened. Instead, both sides said that talks on the wage portion of the contract would reopen with a mediator's help. If those talks failed, the issue would go to arbitration.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown affected about 500,000 passengers and paralyzed travel in a sparsely populated country that spans six time zones, leaving few practical alternatives to flying for many trips.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  unit of the \uc2 Canadian Union of Public Employees\uc2  said in a statement on Saturday afternoon that the contract was rejected by 99.1 percent of the attendants. It added that 94.6 percent of them voted.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Air Canada\uc2  never bargained in good faith on wages,'' \uc2 Wesley Lesosky\uc2 , the president of the \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  unit of the union, said on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  reopened talks with the union after its workers defied a labor board decision declaring the strike illegal. The resulting agreement included, for the first time at the airline, pay for work that flight attendants perform before and after flights, such as boarding passengers and conducting safety checks.\par \par \uc2 The union's initial refusal to end the strike last month followed an effort by the federal government to circumvent a decision by the \uc2 Supreme Court of Canada\uc2  affirming the right of workers to strike even when the action could lead to substantial disruption and inconvenience.\par \par \uc2 Rather than order the workers directly back to work, the government ordered the dispute to be settled with a contract imposed by an arbitrator. The union had objected to that step because arbitrators in Canada rarely introduce new measures, such as payment for work before and after flights, in the contracts they impose.\par \par \uc2 Air Canada\uc2 , however, returned to negotiations with the assistance of a mediator after \uc2 Patty Hajdu\uc2 , the federal labor minister, ordered a review of the airline industry's practice of paying attendants only for the time they spend in the air. The attendants' union and most of the opposition parties in Parliament have long called on the government to change this policy, either through legislation or enforcement of the federal labor code.\par \par \uc2 While the now-rejected agreement establishes the principle of payment for what the union calls ground work, it would not be at full pay. \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  had agreed to pay 70 percent of the regular wage rate for 45 minutes of ground work on smaller aircraft and for 60 minutes on larger planes.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Ross, an associate professor of labor studies at \uc2 McMaster University\uc2  in Hamilton, Ontario, said that the vote against the contract would not jeopardize that gain.\par \par \uc2 ''The employer agreed to that,'' she said. ''That is something they won.''\par \par \uc2 By rejecting the contract, however, the flight attendants, the majority of whom are women, may hope to use arbitration to win on other issues like wages that were not fully resolved in the earlier talks, Professor Ross said. In any case, no imposed settlement can contain less than what is found in the now-rejected agreement.\par \par \uc2 ''The flight attendants are still quite angry,'' she said. ''So you know they might be thinking, 'Hey what do we have to lose? We're going to get ground pay locked in no matter what.'''\par \par \uc2 According to the union, \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  offered a first-year wage increase of 12 percent to flight attendants who have been at the company for five years or less. More experienced attendants would have received an 8 percent raise. The rejected contract also contained annual increases of 3 percent, 2.5 percent and 2.75 percent for the balance of its four-year term.\par \par \uc2 By comparison, last October \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  avoided a strike by pilots that offered a cumulative 42 percent wage increase over four years.\par \par \uc2 While it's possible that some individual flight attendants may take job actions without the union's sanction, Professor Ross said, such actions are likely to be limited. She added that large demonstrations by flight attendants, like those seen before the walkout, are very probable.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250908el980003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
