\par\par \uc2 STOCKS &AMP; BONDS\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Wall St. Sways as It Weighs Whether Job Market Is Too Weak\b0\par\par\uc2 By By The Associated Press \par \uc2 701 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks wobbled lower on Friday as Wall Street questioned whether the U.S. job market has slowed by just enough to get the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut interest rates to help the economy, or by so much that a downturn may be on the way.\par \par \uc2 After rising to an early gain, the S&P 500 erased it and fell 0.3 percent below the high it set the day before. The Dow Jones industrial average dropped 220 points, or 0.5 percent, after swinging between an early gain of nearly 150 points and a loss of 400. The Nasdaq composite edged down by less than 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 The action was more decisive in the bond market, where Treasury yields tumbled after a report from the Labor Department said U.S. employers hired fewer workers in August than economists expected. The government also said that earlier estimates for June and July overstated hiring by 21,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 The disappointing numbers follow last month's discouraging jobs update, along with other lackluster reports in intervening weeks, and traders are now betting on a 100 percent probability that the Fed will cut its main interest rate at its next meeting on Sept. 17, according to data from \uc2 CME Group\uc2 . Investors love such cuts because they can give a kickstart to the economy, but the Fed has held off on them because they can also give inflation more fuel.\par \par \uc2 So far this year, the Fed has been more worried about the potential of inflation worsening because of President Trump's tariffs than about the job market. But Friday's job numbers could push the Fed to consider cutting rates in two weeks by a steeper amount than usual, said \uc2 Brian Jacobsen\uc2 , chief economist at Annex Wealth Management.\par \par \uc2 ''This week has been a story of a slowing labor market, and today's data was the exclamation point,'' according to \uc2 Ellen Zentner\uc2 , chief economic strategist for \uc2 Morgan Stanley Wealth Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Strong hiring for health care jobs had been helping to support the overall market, ''but with it now showing some tangible signs of decline, the foundation underneath the labor market seems to be cracking,'' said \uc2 Rick Rieder\uc2 , chief investment officer of global fixed income at \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While the data on the job market is disappointing, it's still not so weak that it's screaming a recession is here, and the U.S. economy is continuing to grow. A big question for investors is whether the job market can remain in a balance where it's not so strong that it prevents cuts to interest rates but also not so weak that the economy falls off.\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty about that helped lead to Friday's swings in the stock market. Wall Street needs things to go as hoped because it already sent stock prices to records amid expectations for a Goldilocks scenario where interest rates ease, and the economy keeps chugging along.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, Friday's heaviest weight was \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , the chip company that's become the face of the artificial-intelligence boom. It's been contending with criticism that its stock price charged too high, too fast and became too expensive following Wall Street's rush into AI, and it fell 2.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Lululemon\uc2  dropped 18.6 percent after the yoga and athletic gear maker's revenue for the latest quarter fell short of analysts' expectations. \uc2 Calvin McDonald\uc2 , \uc2 Lululemon\uc2 's C.E.O., pointed to disappointing results from its U.S. operation, while chief financial officer \uc2 Meghan Frank\uc2  said \uc2 Lululemon\uc2  is facing ''industrywide challenges, including higher tariff rates.''\par \par \uc2 Still, more stocks rose on Wall Street than fell. Leading the way was \uc2 Broadcom\uc2 , which climbed 9.4 percent after reporting better profit and revenue for the latest quarter than analysts expected. C.E.O. \uc2 Hock Tan\uc2  said customers are continuing to invest strongly in AI chips.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  rose 3.6 percent after proposing a payout plan that could reach $1 trillion for its C.E.O., \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , if the company meets a series of aggressive targets over the next 10 years.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250906el960004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
