\par\par \uc2 STOCKS &AMP; BONDS\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Wall Street Breaks Its Slide as Big Tech Sector Logs Gains\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 688 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Wall Street steadied on Wednesday after \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  and other technology stocks rallied. It also got some relief as pressure eased from the bond market, where the latest discouraging report on the U.S. job market bolstered expectations that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will cut interest rates soon to support the economy.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 climbed 0.5 percent to break the two-day losing slide it had been on since setting its latest high. The Dow Jones industrial average dipped 24 points, or 0.1 percent, and the Nasdaq composite climbed 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 's parent company was one of the strongest forces lifting the market and jumped 9.1 percent after avoiding some of the worst-case scenarios in its antitrust case. A federal judge on Tuesday ordered a shake-up of \uc2 Google\uc2 's search engine but did not force a sale of its Chrome browser.\par \par \uc2 Because \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  is one of Wall Street's most valuable companies, its stock movements carry much more weight on the S&P 500 and other indexes than the typical company's.\par \par \uc2 Also helping to steady Wall Street was a calming bond market. A day earlier, yields climbed worldwide on worries about governments' ability to repay their growing mountains of debt, as well as concerns that President Trump's pressure on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut short-term interest rates could lead to higher inflation in the long term.\par \par \uc2 Such worries have pushed investors to demand higher yields before lending money to governments. And when bonds are paying more in interest, investors feel less need to pay high prices for stocks, which are riskier investments.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Treasury yields retreated after the latest report on the U.S. job market came in weaker than expected. The 10-year Treasury yield fell to 4.22 percent from 4.28 percent late Tuesday, for example.\par \par \uc2 The report showed that U.S. employers were advertising 7.2 million job openings at the end of July, fewer than economists had forecast. The number bolsters the sense on Wall Street that the job market may be ossifying into a low-hire, low-fire state.\par \par \uc2 A weakened job market could push the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to cut its main interest rate for the first time this year at its next meeting, which is scheduled for this month. That's the widespread expectation among traders, with the next big data point coming on Friday via an update on U.S. hiring during August.\par \par \uc2 Lower interest rates could stimulate the job market and overall economy. The downside is that they can also push inflation higher when Mr. Trump's tariffs may be set to raise prices for all kinds of imports.\par \par \uc2 Trading on Wall Street was mixed outside of tech stocks, which benefited from the \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  ruling. \uc2 Apple\uc2  rose 3.8 percent after analysts highlighted how the ruling will still allow it to sign lucrative search deals with \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''This is a relief, an outcome that is much better than feared for \uc2 Google\uc2  and for \uc2 Apple\uc2 ,'' according to \uc2 Chris Marangi\uc2 , co-chief investment officer of value at \uc2 Gabelli Funds\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Macy's\uc2  jumped 20.7 percent for one of the market's bigger gains after the retailer reported stronger profit and revenue for the latest quarter than analysts expected. The owner of Bloomingdale's delivered the best growth in an important measure of sales in three years, and it also raised its forecasts for sales and profit this year.\par \par \uc2 American Bitcoin, a Bitcoin treasury and mining company linked to the Trump family, rose 16.5 percent in a manic first day of trading after completing its merger with Gryphon Digital Mining. Its stock price more than doubled at one point, and its movements were so frenetic that trading was halted several times through the day.\par \par \uc2 Campbell's rose 7.2 percent after the company reported a stronger profit for the latest quarter than analysts expected. It also said, though, that tariffs may help drag its overall earnings lower in its upcoming fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250904el940004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
