\par\par \uc2 Money and Business/Financial Desk; SECTBU\par \b \uc2 The Old Days Are Now Over For Valley Jobs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Conger \par \uc2 1,424 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It's the shut up and grind era, tech workers said, as \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2  and other giants age into large bureaucracies.\par \par \uc2 When Rachel Grey started working at \uc2 Google\uc2  as a software engineer in 2007, it was a good time to be a Noogler, or what the search giant called new employees.\par \par \uc2 At a two-week orientation at \uc2 Google\uc2 's headquarters in Mountain View, Calif., Ms. Grey discovered a utopia of perks. The company's cafeterias served steak and shrimp, kitchens were stocked with fresh juices and gyms offered free workout classes. Workers received stock grants on top of their salaries, a 50 percent match on their retirement contributions and a Christmas bonus that came in the form of $1,000 tucked in an envelope.\par \par \uc2 What also made an impression on Ms. Grey during orientation was that \uc2 Google\uc2  revealed how many machines were in its data centers. ''I saw how transparent things were in the company,'' she said about the normally hush-hush information.\par \par \uc2 Over the years, though, her experience changed as she became a software engineering manager. The Christmas bonus shrank. Employees were no longer provided a fire hose of corporate information. The company abandoned a pledge that its artificial intelligence would not be used for weapons. The budget for promotions dried up, pressuring Ms. Grey to lower performance ratings, which she said was ''stunningly painful.'' In April, just shy of 18 years, the 48-year-old quit what was once her dream job.\par \par \uc2 Life for workers at Silicon Valley's biggest tech companies is different. Very different.\par \par \uc2 Gone are the days when \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 Netflix\uc2  were the dream destinations for tech workers, offering fat salaries, lush corporate campuses and say-anything, do-anything cultures. Now the behemoth firms have aged into large bureaucracies. While many of them still provide free food and pay well, they have little compunction cutting jobs, ordering mandatory office attendance and clamping down on employee debate.\par \par \uc2 It's the shut up and grind era, workers said.\par \par \uc2 ''Tech could still be best in terms of free lunch and a high salary,'' Ms. Grey said, but ''the level of fear has gone way up.''\par \par \uc2 ''I suppose it's better to have lunch and be scared to death than to not have lunch and be scared to death, but I don't know if it's good for you to be there,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Google\uc2  spokeswoman said that many employees had been promoted and that the company had changed its performance management system to better reward high performers. The company has introduced policies meant to encourage employees to focus on their work while also staying true to \uc2 Google\uc2 's goals and culture, she added.\par \par \uc2 As the tech companies became hulking entities with work forces larger than many towns -- and costs to match -- scrutiny increased, too. \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2  and others were driven to make changes as workers and the public questioned their power.\par \par \uc2 The turning point came in 2022 and 2023, when \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  bought \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (renamed X) and shed three-quarters of its employees, while \uc2 Meta\uc2 's chief executive, \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , cut thousands of jobs during what he called ''a year of efficiency.'' \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  also conducted mass layoffs. Many of the companies blamed the pandemic for their overhiring during lockdowns as more people turned to digital services.\par \par \uc2 Along the way, the companies became less tolerant of employee outspokenness. Bosses reasserted themselves after workers protested issues including sexual harassment in the workplace. With the job market flooded with qualified engineers, it became easier to replace those who criticized.\par \par \uc2 ''This is a business, and not a place to act in a way that disrupts co-workers or makes them feel unsafe, to attempt to use the company as a personal platform, or to fight over disruptive issues or debate politics,'' \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 's chief executive, said in a blog post last year.\par \par \uc2 Some would say the changes have simply aligned tech workers with the rest of corporate America, where employees are accustomed to fulfilling corporate priorities.\par \par \uc2 But the shift in tech was compounded by the rise of generative artificial intelligence, which executives say has already made some jobs redundant. In January, Mr. Zuckerberg said he believed A.I. would replace some midlevel engineers this year. Mr. Musk went further, predicting last year that A.I. would eventually eliminate all jobs.\par \par \uc2 ''The tide has definitely turned against tech workers,'' said Catherine Bracy, the founder and chief executive of TechEquity, a nonprofit that pushes for economic inclusion in the industry. ''Companies have even more leverage to use against workers, and A.I. is supercharging that.''\par \par \uc2 Liz Fong-Jones\uc2 , the field chief technology officer at Honeycomb, a San Francisco company that helps engineers find and debug problems in their code, said A.I.'s effect on jobs was overblown. But that could change five years from now, she cautioned.\par \par \uc2 Tech workers could stop A.I. from taking hold, said Ms. Fong-Jones, a former Googler, adding, ''but we're all afraid enough to go along with training our own replacements.''\par \par \uc2 For some tech workers, the change in the workplace was abrupt. Adam Treitler, 32, a human resources strategist who worked at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's New York office before and after Mr. Musk's acquisition, said the company's moves under its new owner were startling.\par \par \uc2 ''From the day before Elon to the day after Elon, it pivoted overnight from 'how do we improve H.R. management' to 'what are the fewest number of steps involved and the fewest number of people needed to pay our employees,''' said Mr. Treitler, who joined \uc2 Twitter\uc2  in 2021 and left in January 2023. He now works for the jewelry company Pandora.\par \par \uc2 X did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Others said the shift had played out more slowly. Ava Sazanami, a designer in her 40s in Seattle, joined \uc2 Meta\uc2  in 2022 to make tools to help users with their privacy settings. The mother of two said she had felt empowered to help solve some of the tech concerns that worried her as a parent.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  also allowed a flexible schedule so she could accompany her children to appointments, and L.G.B.T.Q.-friendly policies made her feel welcome because she had gay family members, she said.\par \par \uc2 But over time, \uc2 Meta\uc2  curtailed its family benefits, Ms. Sazanami said. In January, the company killed its diversity programs and social media policies against hate speech targeting L.G.B.T.Q. people. A month later, she was laid off when \uc2 Meta\uc2  cut 5 percent of its work force.\par \par \uc2 ''We're seeing right now why tech needs unions,'' said Ms. Sazanami, who is looking for a new job. ''The current culture has disempowered workers.''\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokeswoman declined to comment. In an earnings call in January, Mr. Zuckerberg said, ''We operate better as a leaner company.''\par \par \uc2 Some workers are leaving big tech companies to join the A.I. fray. Jason Yuan, 28, started as a designer at \uc2 Apple\uc2 's headquarters in Cupertino, Calif., in 2021. It felt like a lucky break to work for a company that he revered for its design, he said.\par \par \uc2 Yet after the A.I. boom arrived with the release of \uc2 OpenAI\uc2 's ChatGPT chatbot in 2022, Mr. Yuan said, he yearned to get involved. In 2023, he left \uc2 Apple\uc2  to start New Computer, a company that makes a personal companion chatbot. He hopes to work more speedily and make more money, as A.I. is likely to replace him in his lifetime, he said.\par \par \uc2 ''We're reaching the end of our economic life span,'' he said, adding, ''There's a feeling of, I have to make whatever I do now count.''\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 For Ms. Grey, \uc2 Google\uc2 's exhilarating early days seem like another lifetime. The work was not always easy, she said, but the company's culture made it easier to power through. One day, she recalled, she and her co-workers arrived to find Nerf guns on their desks. When a power outage hit, shutting down computers, they grabbed their Nerf guns and broke into a friendly fight.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Google\uc2  had such a glow about it then,'' she said. ''There was an institutionally approved playfulness to it all. I loved that.''\par \par \uc2 Now ''the future of the whole industry seems very shaky,'' said Ms. Grey, who is taking time off from tech.\par \par \uc2 Mike Isaac contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Mike Isaac contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250824el8o0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
